// --- Lesson 1: The San Hunter-Gatherers ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The First People of Southern Africa\n\nThe **San** people are believed to be the **oldest inhabitants** of southern Africa. They have lived in this region for **tens of thousands of years** — long before any other group arrived.\n\nThe San were **hunter-gatherers**, which means they survived by:\n- **Hunting** wild animals (buck, springbok, eland)\n- **Gathering** wild plants, roots, berries, and insects\n\nThey did not farm crops or keep animals. Instead, they moved from place to place following the seasons and the animals. This way of life is called being **nomadic**.\n\n### Where Did the San Live?\n\nThe San once lived across most of southern Africa, from the Cape coast to the Kalahari Desert. Today, small San communities still live in:\n- The **Kalahari Desert** (Botswana and Namibia)\n- Parts of **Namibia** and **South Africa**'),
  q(2, 'What does it mean that the San were "hunter-gatherers"?',
    ['They survived by hunting wild animals and gathering wild plants, roots and berries', 'They farmed crops and raised cattle', 'They built large cities and traded with Europe', 'They fished in the ocean and gathered seashells'], 0,
    'Hunter-gatherers do not farm or keep livestock. The San hunted wild animals like eland and springbok, and gathered wild foods like berries, roots, nuts, and even insects like locusts and caterpillars.'),
  t(3, '## How the San Lived\n\n### Social Organisation\n\nThe San lived in **small bands** (groups) of about **25 to 50 people**. These bands were made up of extended families.\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Leadership** | No chief — decisions were made by the whole group through discussion |\n| **Sharing** | All food was shared equally among the band |\n| **Movement** | Bands moved regularly to follow water, animals, and seasonal foods |\n| **Shelters** | Simple shelters made from branches and grass, easy to build and leave behind |\n\n### Hunting\n\n- Men usually did the hunting\n- They used **bows and arrows** tipped with **poison** (made from plants or beetle larvae)\n- They tracked animals for hours or even days, reading footprints and other signs\n- **Snares** (traps) were also used for smaller animals\n\n### Gathering\n\n- Women and children gathered plant foods\n- This included **roots, bulbs, berries, nuts, wild melons**, and **insects**\n- Gathering provided about **80% of the food** the San ate\n- Women used a **digging stick** to dig up roots and bulbs\n- They carried food in bags made from animal skin'),
  fb(4, 'The San lived in small groups called ___. Women used a ___ to dig up roots and bulbs.',
    ['bands', 'digging stick'],
    'San bands were small family groups of about 25-50 people. Women were the main food providers — gathering made up about 80% of the San diet. The digging stick was their most important tool for finding underground roots and bulbs.'),
  t(5, '## San Rock Art\n\nThe San are famous for their **rock art** — paintings and engravings found on rock surfaces and in caves across southern Africa. There are **tens of thousands** of rock art sites in South Africa alone.\n\n### What Did They Paint?\n\n| Subject | Examples |\n|---------|----------|\n| **Animals** | Eland (their most sacred animal), elephants, rhinos, buck |\n| **Hunting scenes** | Men with bows chasing animals |\n| **People dancing** | The trance dance (healing dance) |\n| **Half-human, half-animal figures** | Believed to show spiritual experiences |\n| **Handprints** | Made by blowing paint over a hand pressed against the rock |\n\n### How Was the Paint Made?\n\nThe San made paint from natural materials:\n- **Red and brown**: iron oxide (ochre)\n- **White**: bird droppings or white clay\n- **Black**: charcoal or manganese oxide\n- **Binder**: animal fat or blood mixed with the pigment\n\nRock art was not just decoration — it had deep **spiritual meaning**. Many paintings show the **trance dance**, a ceremony where healers entered a trance to communicate with the spirit world and heal sick people.'),
  q(6, 'Which animal appears most often in San rock art and was considered sacred?',
    ['The eland — it was the San\'s most sacred animal and appears in many paintings', 'The lion — because it was the most dangerous predator', 'The elephant — because it was the largest animal', 'The fish — because the San lived near rivers'], 0,
    'The eland was the most important animal in San spiritual life. They believed the eland had great spiritual power (potency). It appears in rock art more than any other animal and was central to many San rituals, including the trance dance and coming-of-age ceremonies.'),
  t(7, '## San Beliefs and the Trance Dance\n\nThe San had a rich **spiritual life**. They believed in a spirit world that existed alongside the physical world.\n\n### Key Beliefs\n\n- A **creator god** who made the world and all living things\n- **Spirits** of the dead who could influence the living\n- The **eland** was the most spiritually powerful animal\n- Certain people called **healers** (or shamans) could enter the spirit world through a special dance\n\n### The Trance Dance (Healing Dance)\n\nThe trance dance was the most important ritual of the San:\n\n1. The **whole band** gathered — men, women, and children\n2. **Women sat in a circle** clapping and singing special songs\n3. **Healers danced** around the circle for hours, stomping their feet\n4. The dancing and music helped healers enter a **trance** (altered state of consciousness)\n5. In the trance, healers believed they could **heal the sick**, bring **rain**, and communicate with **spirits**\n6. Healers sometimes got **nosebleeds** during the trance — this was seen as a sign of spiritual power\n\nMany San rock paintings show the trance dance, with figures bending forward, nosebleeding, or transforming into half-animal beings.'),
  fb(8, 'The San\'s most important ritual was the ___ dance. During this ceremony, ___ entered a trance to heal the sick and communicate with spirits.',
    ['trance', 'healers'],
    'The trance dance (also called the healing dance) was central to San spiritual life. Healers (shamans) danced for hours to rhythmic clapping and singing until they entered an altered state of consciousness. They believed this allowed them to draw out sickness and communicate with the spirit world.'),
  q(9, 'How did the San make decisions in their bands?',
    ['Through group discussion — there was no chief and everyone had a say', 'A king or chief made all the decisions alone', 'Only the oldest man could make decisions', 'They voted by raising their hands'], 0,
    'The San had an egalitarian society, meaning everyone was considered equal. There were no chiefs or kings. Important decisions were made through group discussion where everyone could share their views. Elders were respected for their wisdom, but they did not have absolute authority.'),
  t(10, '## Why the San Way of Life Changed\n\nThe San way of life has been under threat for hundreds of years:\n\n- About **2,000 years ago**, **Khoikhoi herders** moved into San territory with their livestock\n- Later, **Bantu-speaking farmers** arrived from further north, bringing cattle and crops\n- From the **1600s**, European **colonists** (Dutch and British) took San land for farming\n- The San were **hunted, killed, and driven off their land**\n- Many San were forced to work on farms as labourers\n- Their hunting grounds were turned into **farms and towns**\n\nToday, very few San people still live as hunter-gatherers. Most have had to adapt to modern life. However, their **rock art, language (with click sounds), and cultural knowledge** are treasured as part of South Africa\'s heritage.\n\nSouth Africa\'s national motto, **!ke e: /xarra //ke** ("Diverse People Unite"), is written in the San /Xam language — a tribute to the first people of southern Africa.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The Khoikhoi Herders ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Who Were the Khoikhoi?\n\nThe **Khoikhoi** (meaning "people of people" or "the real people") were **pastoralists** — people who kept and herded livestock. They arrived in southern Africa about **2,000 years ago**.\n\nUnlike the San, the Khoikhoi:\n- Kept **cattle** and **fat-tailed sheep**\n- Lived in **larger, more settled communities**\n- Had a **social hierarchy** with chiefs and councils\n\n### San and Khoikhoi — The Khoisan\n\nTogether, the San and Khoikhoi are sometimes called the **Khoisan** people because they share:\n- Similar **click languages** (using sounds made by clicking the tongue)\n- Similar **physical features**\n- Overlapping **territory** in southern Africa\n\nHowever, they had very different ways of life:\n\n| Feature | San | Khoikhoi |\n|---------|-----|----------|\n| **Economy** | Hunting and gathering | Herding cattle and sheep |\n| **Settlement** | Nomadic (always moving) | Semi-nomadic (moved with herds but had more permanent camps) |\n| **Food** | Wild plants and animals | Milk, meat from livestock, and some gathered foods |\n| **Social structure** | Small bands, no chief | Larger clans with chiefs |\n| **Possessions** | Very few belongings | Cattle were a sign of wealth |'),
  q(2, 'What is the main difference between how the San and Khoikhoi lived?',
    ['The San were hunter-gatherers who moved constantly, while the Khoikhoi were herders who kept cattle and sheep', 'The San lived in cities while the Khoikhoi lived in forests', 'The Khoikhoi hunted whales while the San farmed maize', 'There was no difference — they lived exactly the same way'], 0,
    'The key difference was their economy: the San survived by hunting wild animals and gathering wild plants (hunter-gatherers), while the Khoikhoi kept domesticated animals like cattle and sheep (pastoralists/herders). This meant the Khoikhoi could stay in one area longer and accumulate wealth in the form of livestock.'),
  t(3, '## How the Khoikhoi Lived\n\n### Settlements\n\nThe Khoikhoi lived in dome-shaped huts called **matjieshuise** (mat houses). These were:\n- Made from a **frame of bent poles** covered with **woven reed mats**\n- Easy to **take apart and carry** when the group moved\n- Arranged in a **circle** around a central area where cattle were kept at night (the **kraal**)\n\n### Cattle — The Centre of Khoikhoi Life\n\nCattle were extremely important to the Khoikhoi:\n\n| Use of Cattle | Detail |\n|---------------|--------|\n| **Food** | Milk was a daily food; meat was eaten on special occasions |\n| **Wealth** | A person\'s wealth was measured by how many cattle they owned |\n| **Bride price (lobola)** | Cattle were given to the bride\'s family at marriage |\n| **Trade** | Cattle were traded with other groups |\n| **Status** | Chiefs and wealthy men owned the most cattle |\n| **Transport** | Cattle were used as pack animals to carry belongings when moving |\n\n### Social Structure\n\n- Khoikhoi society was divided into **clans** (groups of related families)\n- Each clan had a **chief** (kaptein) who led with the help of a **council of elders**\n- Chiefs settled disputes, made decisions about moving, and managed trade\n- Women gathered wild plants and prepared food; men herded cattle and hunted'),
  fb(4, 'Khoikhoi dome-shaped huts were called ___. Cattle were kept at night in a central enclosure called a ___.',
    ['matjieshuise', 'kraal'],
    'Matjieshuise (mat houses) were practical dwellings made from bent poles covered with woven reed mats. They could be quickly dismantled and carried when the group needed to move to fresh grazing land. The kraal was the circular enclosure in the middle of the settlement where cattle were kept safe at night from predators and thieves.'),
  t(5, '## Khoikhoi Trade and Interaction\n\nThe Khoikhoi were active **traders**. They traded with:\n\n### Other African Groups\n- Traded **cattle, sheep, and animal skins** with Bantu-speaking farmers in exchange for **iron tools, copper, and tobacco**\n- Traded with the San — Khoikhoi would exchange milk and meat for wild honey and animal skins\n\n### European Ships (from the late 1400s)\n- When Portuguese, Dutch, and English ships stopped at the Cape, the Khoikhoi traded:\n  - **Cattle and sheep** for **iron, copper, brass, tobacco, and beads**\n- The Khoikhoi at the Cape became experienced traders who knew the value of their goods\n\n### The Khoikhoi and the Dutch at the Cape\n\nIn **1652**, the Dutch East India Company (VOC) established a refreshment station at the Cape under **Jan van Riebeeck**. At first, the relationship was based on trade. But soon:\n\n- The Dutch wanted **more and more land** for farming\n- They began to **take Khoikhoi grazing land**\n- The Khoikhoi fought back in the **Khoikhoi-Dutch Wars** (1659-1660 and 1673-1677)\n- The Khoikhoi lost these wars and much of their land\n- Many Khoikhoi died from **European diseases** (especially smallpox) to which they had no immunity'),
  q(6, 'Why did conflict develop between the Khoikhoi and the Dutch settlers at the Cape?',
    ['The Dutch took more and more Khoikhoi grazing land for their own farming', 'The Khoikhoi refused to sell any cattle at all', 'The Dutch wanted to learn the Khoikhoi language', 'The Khoikhoi attacked Dutch ships for no reason'], 0,
    'The conflict arose because the Dutch settlers at the Cape needed farmland to grow food for passing ships. They expanded into Khoikhoi grazing territory, taking the land the Khoikhoi needed for their cattle herds. This led to two wars (1659-1660 and 1673-1677), both of which the Khoikhoi lost.'),
  t(7, '## What Happened to the Khoikhoi?\n\nThe Khoikhoi way of life was devastated by colonialism:\n\n| Cause | Effect |\n|-------|--------|\n| **Loss of land** | Dutch settlers took their grazing land, leaving them nowhere to herd cattle |\n| **Smallpox epidemics** | In 1713, a devastating smallpox epidemic killed thousands of Khoikhoi — they had no immunity to European diseases |\n| **Loss of cattle** | Without land, they could not keep cattle — their main source of wealth and food |\n| **Forced labour** | Many Khoikhoi were forced to work on Dutch farms as servants and labourers |\n| **Loss of independence** | By the 1700s, the Khoikhoi at the Cape had lost their independence as a people |\n\n### The Khoikhoi Legacy\n\nDespite these losses, the Khoikhoi left a lasting legacy:\n\n- Many **place names** in South Africa come from Khoikhoi languages (e.g., Karoo, Knysna, Tsitsikamma)\n- The **Afrikaans language** includes words borrowed from Khoikhoi\n- **Click sounds** in some South African languages (like Xhosa and Zulu) were adopted from the Khoisan\n- Khoikhoi **descendants** (often called the Cape Khoi) still live in South Africa and work to preserve their culture and land rights'),
  fb(8, 'In 1713, thousands of Khoikhoi were killed by a devastating ___ epidemic. Many Khoikhoi place names survive today, such as ___ and Knysna.',
    ['smallpox', 'Karoo'],
    'The smallpox epidemic of 1713 was catastrophic for the Khoikhoi because they had never been exposed to this European disease and had no natural immunity. Place names like Karoo (meaning "land of thirst"), Knysna, and Tsitsikamma are all from Khoikhoi languages and remind us of the Khoisan people\'s deep connection to the land.'),
  q(9, 'Which of these is a lasting legacy of the Khoikhoi people in South Africa?',
    ['Many South African place names come from Khoikhoi languages, and click sounds were adopted into Xhosa and Zulu', 'They built the first cities in South Africa', 'They invented the Afrikaans language entirely', 'They introduced cattle farming to Europe'], 0,
    'The Khoikhoi left a lasting linguistic and cultural legacy. Place names like Karoo, Knysna, and Tsitsikamma come from Khoikhoi languages. The click consonants found in Xhosa, Zulu, and other Nguni languages were adopted from contact with the Khoisan peoples. Even Afrikaans contains some Khoikhoi-origin words.'),
  t(10, '## Remembering the Khoisan Today\n\nThe Khoisan (San and Khoikhoi together) are recognised as the **first peoples** of southern Africa.\n\n### How South Africa Remembers the Khoisan\n\n- South Africa\'s **national motto** is written in the /Xam San language: **!ke e: /xarra //ke** ("Diverse People Unite")\n- The **coat of arms** features two San-style human figures\n- **Heritage sites** such as the rock art in the uKhahlamba-Drakensberg Park (a UNESCO World Heritage Site) preserve San art\n- The government has recognised the need to **restore Khoisan land rights** and cultural heritage\n- In **2019**, the Traditional and Khoi-San Leadership Act was signed into law, giving official recognition to Khoisan leaders\n\nThe story of the San and Khoikhoi reminds us that South Africa\'s history stretches back thousands of years — long before the arrival of European colonists. Their way of life, art, and language are precious parts of our shared heritage.'),
];

