// --- Lesson 1: The Land of the Nile ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Ancient Egypt — A Great African Civilisation\n\n**Ancient Egypt** was one of the greatest civilisations in history. It lasted for over **3,000 years** (from about 3100 BCE to 30 BCE) and was located in **north-eastern Africa** along the **Nile River**.\n\nAncient Egypt was an **African** civilisation — this is important to remember because Egypt is sometimes wrongly separated from the rest of Africa.\n\n### The Nile River — Egypt\'s Lifeline\n\nThe **Nile** is the **longest river in Africa** (about 6,650 km). Ancient Egypt would not have existed without it.\n\n| Gift of the Nile | How It Helped |\n|-------------------|---------------|\n| **Annual flooding** | Every year the Nile flooded, depositing rich black silt on the land — perfect for farming |\n| **Fertile soil** | The "Black Land" (Kemet) along the Nile was ideal for growing wheat, barley, and flax |\n| **Water for irrigation** | Farmers built canals and channels (shaduf) to bring water to their fields |\n| **Transport** | Boats carried goods and people up and down the river |\n| **Papyrus** | Papyrus reeds growing along the Nile were used to make paper, boats, and baskets |\n| **Fish and birds** | The Nile provided food from fishing and bird hunting |\n\nThe Greek historian **Herodotus** called Egypt "the gift of the Nile" — without the river, Egypt would have been nothing but desert.'),
  q(2, 'Why did the Greek historian Herodotus call Egypt "the gift of the Nile"?',
    ['Because the Nile provided water, fertile soil, transport, and food — without it, Egypt would be desert', 'Because the Nile was the only river in Africa', 'Because the Greeks gave the Nile to Egypt as a gift', 'Because Egypt gave the Nile to Greece as a present'], 0,
    'Herodotus recognised that the entire Egyptian civilisation depended on the Nile River. The annual floods brought rich black silt that made the land fertile for farming. The river also provided water, transport, papyrus, fish, and was the reason people settled there in the first place. Beyond the Nile valley was only desert.'),
  t(3, '## Pharaohs — The God-Kings of Egypt\n\nAncient Egypt was ruled by **pharaohs** — powerful kings (and sometimes queens) who were believed to be **living gods**.\n\n### The Role of the Pharaoh\n\n| Responsibility | Detail |\n|----------------|--------|\n| **Political leader** | Made all the laws, commanded the army, and collected taxes |\n| **Religious leader** | Performed religious ceremonies and was seen as the link between the gods and the people |\n| **Owner of all land** | All the land in Egypt belonged to the pharaoh |\n| **Commander of the army** | Led Egypt in times of war |\n| **Judge** | Settled disputes and ensured justice |\n\n### Famous Pharaohs\n\n- **Khufu** (Cheops) — Built the Great Pyramid of Giza, the largest pyramid ever constructed\n- **Hatshepsut** — One of the few female pharaohs; she ruled successfully for over 20 years and built a magnificent temple at Deir el-Bahri\n- **Tutankhamun** — The "boy king" who became pharaoh at age 9; his tomb was discovered almost intact in 1922\n- **Ramesses II** (Ramesses the Great) — One of the most powerful pharaohs; built the temples at Abu Simbel\n- **Cleopatra VII** — The last pharaoh of Egypt; ruled before Egypt became part of the Roman Empire'),
  fb(4, 'Pharaohs were believed to be living ___. The Great Pyramid of Giza was built by the pharaoh ___.',
    ['gods', 'Khufu'],
    'The pharaoh was not just a political leader but also a religious figure. Ancient Egyptians believed the pharaoh was a living god — the link between the human world and the world of the gods. Khufu (also known by his Greek name Cheops) built the Great Pyramid of Giza around 2560 BCE. It is the only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still standing.'),
  t(5, '## The Pyramids and Mummification\n\n### Why Were the Pyramids Built?\n\nThe pyramids were **tombs** — massive burial monuments built for pharaohs and important people. The ancient Egyptians believed in an **afterlife** and wanted to preserve the body for the journey to the next world.\n\n### The Great Pyramid of Giza\n\n| Fact | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **Built for** | Pharaoh Khufu |\n| **Date** | About 2560 BCE |\n| **Height** | 146 metres (originally) — the tallest building in the world for over 3,800 years |\n| **Blocks** | Made of about 2.3 million stone blocks, each weighing about 2.5 tonnes |\n| **Workers** | Built by thousands of skilled workers and labourers (NOT slaves, as was once believed) |\n\n### Mummification — Preserving the Dead\n\nThe Egyptians developed **mummification** to preserve the body for the afterlife. The process took about **70 days**:\n\n1. The body was washed and purified\n2. The **brain** was removed through the nose using a hook\n3. The **internal organs** (lungs, liver, stomach, intestines) were removed and placed in special jars called **canopic jars**\n4. The **heart** was left in place — Egyptians believed it would be weighed in the afterlife\n5. The body was covered in **natron** (a natural salt) to dry it out for 40 days\n6. The body was stuffed with linen and sawdust, then wrapped in layers of **linen bandages**\n7. **Amulets** (charms) were placed between the bandages for protection\n8. A **funeral mask** was placed over the face\n9. The mummy was placed in a **coffin** (sarcophagus) inside the tomb'),
  q(6, 'Why did the ancient Egyptians mummify their dead?',
    ['They believed the body needed to be preserved for the person\'s journey to the afterlife', 'They wanted to frighten their enemies', 'They did not know how to bury bodies underground', 'It was a punishment for criminals'], 0,
    'The ancient Egyptians believed in an afterlife — a life after death. They believed the person\'s spirit (ka and ba) needed a preserved body to return to. If the body decayed, the spirit would be lost. Mummification preserved the body so the person could live forever in the afterlife.'),
  t(7, '## Egyptian Society\n\nAncient Egyptian society was organised like a **pyramid**, with the pharaoh at the top and the largest groups at the bottom.\n\n### Social Hierarchy\n\n| Level | Who | Role |\n|-------|-----|------|\n| **1 (top)** | **Pharaoh** | Absolute ruler, living god |\n| **2** | **Priests and nobles** | Ran temples, served the gods, managed government |\n| **3** | **Scribes and officials** | Kept records, collected taxes, managed building projects |\n| **4** | **Artisans and craftspeople** | Made pottery, jewellery, tools, furniture, and built monuments |\n| **5** | **Farmers** | Grew crops on the fertile Nile banks — the majority of the population |\n| **6 (bottom)** | **Slaves** | Prisoners of war who did manual labour |\n\n### Daily Life\n\n- **Food**: Bread, beer, onions, garlic, fish, figs, dates, and occasionally meat\n- **Clothing**: Light linen clothing due to the hot climate; both men and women wore **eye makeup** (kohl)\n- **Homes**: Made of mud bricks, with flat roofs used as extra living space\n- **Children**: Boys from wealthy families went to school to become scribes; girls learned household skills at home\n- **Entertainment**: Music, dancing, board games (especially **senet**), hunting, and fishing'),
  fb(8, 'Egyptian scribes kept records using a writing system called ___. Farmers grew crops on the fertile banks of the ___ River.',
    ['hieroglyphics', 'Nile'],
    'Hieroglyphics was the ancient Egyptian writing system using picture symbols. Scribes were highly respected because reading and writing were rare skills. Most of Egypt\'s population were farmers who depended on the annual flooding of the Nile to water and fertilise their land.'),
  q(9, 'Who made up the largest group in ancient Egyptian society?',
    ['Farmers who grew crops on the fertile banks of the Nile', 'Pharaohs and their families', 'Priests who ran the temples', 'Scribes who kept government records'], 0,
    'Like most ancient societies, the majority of people in Egypt were farmers. They grew wheat, barley, flax, and vegetables on the fertile land along the Nile. They paid taxes (usually in grain) to the pharaoh and could be called upon for building projects during the flood season when they could not farm.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Hieroglyphics, Gods, and Beliefs ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Hieroglyphics — The Writing of the Gods\n\n**Hieroglyphics** was the writing system of ancient Egypt. The word means "sacred carvings" in Greek.\n\n### How Hieroglyphics Worked\n\n- Used over **700 picture symbols** (hieroglyphs)\n- Each symbol could represent a **sound**, a **word**, or an **idea**\n- Written on **temple walls, tombs, monuments**, and on **papyrus** (a paper-like material made from the papyrus plant)\n- Could be written **left to right, right to left, or top to bottom** — you can tell the direction by looking at which way the animal and human figures face\n\n### Who Could Read and Write?\n\n- Only **scribes** could read and write — they spent years in special schools learning\n- Scribes were **highly respected** and did not have to do manual labour or pay taxes\n- They wrote with **reed pens** dipped in black or red ink\n- A simpler script called **hieratic** was used for everyday writing; **demotic** was an even simpler later form\n\n### The Rosetta Stone\n\nFor centuries, nobody could read hieroglyphics. In **1799**, French soldiers in Egypt found the **Rosetta Stone** — a stone slab with the same text written in three scripts: hieroglyphics, demotic, and **Greek**. In **1822**, the Frenchman **Jean-Fran\u00e7ois Champollion** used the Greek text to finally decode hieroglyphics.'),
  q(2, 'How was the mystery of hieroglyphics finally solved?',
    ['The Rosetta Stone, found in 1799, had the same text in three scripts, allowing Champollion to decode hieroglyphics using the Greek translation', 'An Egyptian pharaoh wrote a dictionary before he died', 'Scientists used computers to translate the symbols', 'A scribe taught European visitors how to read the symbols'], 0,
    'The Rosetta Stone was the key to unlocking hieroglyphics. It contained the same decree written in hieroglyphics, demotic script, and Greek. Since scholars could read Greek, Jean-Fran\u00e7ois Champollion was able to match the Greek words to the hieroglyphic symbols and crack the code in 1822.'),
  t(3, '## Egyptian Gods and Religion\n\nThe ancient Egyptians worshipped **hundreds of gods and goddesses**. Religion was central to every part of Egyptian life.\n\n### Major Gods and Goddesses\n\n| God/Goddess | Role | Appearance |\n|-------------|------|------------|\n| **Ra** (or Re) | Sun god — the most important god; creator of all life | Man with a falcon head and a sun disc |\n| **Osiris** | God of the dead and the afterlife; ruler of the underworld | Green-skinned man wrapped in mummy bandages |\n| **Isis** | Goddess of magic and motherhood; wife of Osiris | Woman with a throne on her head |\n| **Horus** | God of the sky and protector of the pharaoh; son of Osiris and Isis | Man with a falcon head |\n| **Anubis** | God of mummification and the dead | Man with a jackal head |\n| **Thoth** | God of wisdom, writing, and knowledge | Man with an ibis head |\n| **Bastet** | Goddess of home, cats, and protection | Woman with a cat head |\n\n### Temples\n\n- Temples were the **homes of the gods** — not places of public worship\n- Only **priests** could enter the inner chambers\n- Priests performed daily rituals: washing, dressing, and feeding the god\'s statue\n- The most famous temples include **Karnak** and **Luxor** (dedicated to Amun-Ra)'),
  fb(4, 'The sun god ___ was considered the most important Egyptian god. The god of mummification and the dead was ___, who had the head of a jackal.',
    ['Ra', 'Anubis'],
    'Ra (the sun god) was central to Egyptian religion because the sun was seen as the source of all life. Anubis was the jackal-headed god who oversaw mummification and guided the dead to the afterlife. He was also believed to weigh the heart of the dead person against the feather of truth.'),
  t(5, '## The Afterlife — The Book of the Dead\n\nThe Egyptians believed in a detailed **afterlife**. When a person died, their spirit would journey to the **underworld** ruled by Osiris.\n\n### The Weighing of the Heart\n\nThe most important moment in the afterlife was the **weighing of the heart ceremony**:\n\n1. The dead person\'s **heart** was placed on one side of a scale\n2. The **feather of Ma\'at** (truth and justice) was placed on the other side\n3. **Anubis** operated the scale\n4. **Thoth** recorded the result\n5. If the heart was **lighter than or equal to the feather** — the person had lived a good life and was allowed into the afterlife (the Field of Reeds)\n6. If the heart was **heavier** — it meant the person had done evil, and the heart was eaten by **Ammit** (a terrifying creature — part crocodile, part lion, part hippo)\n\n### The Book of the Dead\n\n- A collection of **spells, prayers, and instructions** written on papyrus scrolls\n- Placed inside the coffin with the mummy to guide the dead person through the underworld\n- Included spells to protect against dangers, pass tests, and speak to the gods\n- Rich people had beautifully illustrated scrolls; poorer people had simpler versions'),
  q(6, 'What happened to a person\'s heart if it was heavier than the feather of Ma\'at?',
    ['The heart was eaten by Ammit, a monster, meaning the person would not enter the afterlife', 'The person was sent back to life for a second chance', 'The pharaoh would pardon them', 'Nothing happened — everyone passed the test'], 0,
    'The weighing of the heart was the ultimate judgement. If the heart was heavy with sin and evil deeds, it would outweigh the feather of Ma\'at (truth). Ammit — a terrifying creature with the head of a crocodile, the body of a lion, and the hindquarters of a hippo — would devour the heart, and the person would cease to exist forever.'),
  t(7, '## Egyptian Inventions and Achievements\n\nAncient Egypt made remarkable contributions that still influence us today.\n\n### Key Achievements\n\n| Achievement | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| **Calendar** | The Egyptians created a 365-day calendar based on the Nile\'s annual flood and the star Sirius |\n| **Mathematics** | Used a decimal system; calculated areas and volumes for building projects |\n| **Medicine** | Egyptian doctors set broken bones, performed surgery, and used herbal remedies; they wrote the world\'s first medical textbooks |\n| **Architecture** | The pyramids and temples are engineering marvels that still stand today |\n| **Paper (papyrus)** | Made from the papyrus plant — the first practical writing material |\n| **Irrigation** | Built canals, dams, and the shaduf (a lever system) to bring water to fields |\n| **Glass and pottery** | Pioneered glassmaking and created beautiful ceramic works |\n| **Cosmetics** | Invented eye makeup (kohl) which also protected eyes from the sun and insects |\n\n### Egypt\'s Lasting Legacy\n\n- The pyramids are one of the **Seven Wonders of the Ancient World**\n- Egyptian art, architecture, and beliefs have influenced cultures for thousands of years\n- The study of ancient Egypt is called **Egyptology**\n- Egypt reminds us that **Africa is the cradle of civilisation** — great kingdoms and achievements began right here on our continent'),
  fb(8, 'The Egyptians created a ___-day calendar. Writing material made from a plant growing along the Nile is called ___.',
    ['365', 'papyrus'],
    'The Egyptian calendar had 365 days, divided into 12 months of 30 days plus 5 extra days. It was based on the annual flooding of the Nile and the heliacal rising of the star Sirius. Papyrus was made by cutting thin strips of the papyrus plant\'s stem, laying them in overlapping layers, pressing, and drying them into sheets.'),
  q(9, 'Why is it important to remember that ancient Egypt was an African civilisation?',
    ['Because it shows that great civilisations, inventions, and achievements began in Africa — Africa is the cradle of civilisation', 'Because Egypt is on a different continent from Africa', 'Because only European countries had great civilisations', 'Because the pharaohs were European'], 0,
    'Ancient Egypt is sometimes wrongly separated from the rest of Africa in history books. But Egypt is in Africa, and its civilisation was built by Africans. Recognising this helps correct the false idea that great civilisations only developed in Europe or Asia. Africa is the cradle of humanity and of some of the world\'s oldest and greatest civilisations.'),
  t(10, '## Summary: Ancient Egypt\n\nAncient Egypt was one of the most remarkable civilisations in human history, and it was African.\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- The **Nile River** made Egyptian civilisation possible by providing water, fertile soil, and transport\n- **Pharaohs** were powerful god-kings who ruled for over 3,000 years\n- The **pyramids** and **mummification** reflect Egyptian beliefs about the afterlife\n- **Hieroglyphics** was a complex writing system decoded only in 1822 thanks to the Rosetta Stone\n- Egyptian **religion** centred on hundreds of gods, temples, and the belief in an afterlife\n- Egyptian achievements in **medicine, mathematics, architecture, and writing** influenced the entire world\n\nAs South Africans studying African history, we can be proud that the continent produced one of the greatest civilisations the world has ever known.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 3 (History – Term 2): An Ancient Society: China
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: The Great Wall and the Silk Road ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Ancient China — A Civilisation of Inventions\n\n**Ancient China** is one of the world\'s oldest civilisations, stretching back over **4,000 years**. Like Egypt, it grew along a great river — the **Huang He** (Yellow River).\n\n### Geography\n\n| Feature | Importance |\n|---------|------------|\n| **Huang He (Yellow River)** | Provided fertile soil for farming; called the "cradle of Chinese civilisation" |\n| **Yangtze River** | China\'s longest river; important for farming rice and for transport |\n| **Mountains and deserts** | The Himalayas, Gobi Desert, and other barriers protected China from invasion but also isolated it |\n| **Coastline** | The east coast allowed fishing and later trade |\n\n### Dynasties\n\nChina was ruled by a series of **dynasties** — families who ruled for many generations. When one family lost power, a new dynasty took over. This is called the **dynastic cycle**.\n\n| Dynasty | Approximate Dates | Key Achievements |\n|---------|--------------------|-------------------|\n| **Shang** | 1600–1046 BCE | First Chinese writing, bronze working |\n| **Zhou** | 1046–256 BCE | Philosophy (Confucius), iron tools |\n| **Qin** | 221–206 BCE | First emperor (Qin Shi Huang), Great Wall, standardised writing, weights, measures |\n| **Han** | 206 BCE–220 CE | Silk Road, paper, golden age of culture |'),
  q(2, 'Which river is known as the "cradle of Chinese civilisation"?',
    ['The Huang He (Yellow River) — it provided fertile soil for early Chinese farming communities', 'The Nile River', 'The Yangtze River', 'The Amazon River'], 0,
    'The Huang He (Yellow River) earned this name because the earliest Chinese civilisation developed along its banks. The river deposited rich yellow silt (loess) on the surrounding land, making it excellent for growing crops like millet and wheat. However, it also flooded frequently, earning it the nickname "China\'s Sorrow."'),
  t(3, '## The First Emperor and the Great Wall\n\n### Qin Shi Huang — The First Emperor\n\nIn **221 BCE**, a powerful ruler named **Qin Shi Huang** conquered all the rival states and united China for the first time. He called himself the **First Emperor** (Shi Huangdi).\n\n| Achievement | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| **United China** | Brought all the warring states together under one ruler |\n| **Standardised writing** | All of China used the same writing system |\n| **Standardised weights and measures** | Made trade easier across the empire |\n| **Built roads and canals** | Connected the empire and improved transport |\n| **Started the Great Wall** | Connected existing walls into one continuous barrier against northern invaders |\n| **Terracotta Army** | Built an army of over 8,000 life-sized clay soldiers to guard his tomb |\n\n### The Great Wall of China\n\nThe **Great Wall** is one of the most famous structures in the world:\n- **Purpose**: To protect China from invasion by northern nomadic tribes (the Xiongnu)\n- **Length**: Over **21,000 km** in total (including all branches and sections built over centuries)\n- **Construction**: Built and extended by many dynasties over about 2,000 years\n- **Materials**: Rammed earth, stone, and brick\n- **Workers**: Hundreds of thousands of workers — soldiers, peasants, and prisoners. Many died during construction\n- **Watch towers**: Built at regular intervals for soldiers to keep lookout\n\nThe Great Wall is a **UNESCO World Heritage Site** and one of the most recognisable landmarks in the world.'),
  fb(4, 'China\'s first emperor was ___. He began building the ___ to protect China from northern invaders.',
    ['Qin Shi Huang', 'Great Wall'],
    'Qin Shi Huang united China in 221 BCE and began many major projects, including connecting existing defensive walls into what would become the Great Wall of China. He also standardised writing, weights, and measures across the empire. His tomb, guarded by the famous Terracotta Army, was discovered in 1974.'),
  t(5, '## The Silk Road — Connecting East and West\n\nThe **Silk Road** was not a single road but a **network of trade routes** that connected China to the Mediterranean world (Rome, Persia, India, and the Middle East).\n\n### Key Facts\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Name** | Named after **silk** — China\'s most valuable export, which was kept a closely guarded secret |\n| **Length** | About **6,400 km** from China to the Mediterranean |\n| **Period** | Most active during the **Han Dynasty** (about 130 BCE onward) |\n| **Route** | From Chang\'an (Xi\'an) in China, through Central Asia, Persia, to the Mediterranean |\n\n### What Was Traded?\n\n| From China (East) | From the West |\n|-------------------|---------------|\n| Silk | Gold and silver |\n| Tea | Glassware |\n| Porcelain (china) | Wool and linen |\n| Paper | Precious stones |\n| Spices | Horses |\n| Gunpowder (later) | Grapes and wine |\n\n### More Than Trade\n\nThe Silk Road carried more than goods — it also spread:\n- **Ideas**: Buddhism spread from India to China along the Silk Road\n- **Technology**: Papermaking and gunpowder spread westward\n- **Diseases**: The Black Death (bubonic plague) may have spread along the Silk Road to Europe\n- **Culture**: Art, music, and food were exchanged between civilisations'),
  q(6, 'Why was the Silk Road important for more than just trade?',
    ['It also spread ideas, religions, technology, and culture between East and West', 'It was the only road in the ancient world', 'It was made entirely of silk fabric', 'It was important only for selling silk to Rome'], 0,
    'The Silk Road was a conduit for the exchange of much more than goods. Buddhism spread from India to China along these routes. Chinese inventions like paper and gunpowder eventually reached Europe. Art, music, food, languages, and even diseases (like the Black Death) all travelled along the Silk Road, connecting distant civilisations.'),
  t(7, '## The Terracotta Army\n\nOne of the most incredible archaeological discoveries of the 20th century is the **Terracotta Army** of Qin Shi Huang.\n\n### Discovery\n\nIn **1974**, farmers digging a well near the city of **Xi\'an** accidentally uncovered clay figures. Archaeologists then spent years excavating one of the most remarkable finds in history.\n\n### What Was Found\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Number of figures** | Over **8,000** life-sized terracotta (baked clay) soldiers, plus horses and chariots |\n| **Purpose** | To guard the emperor\'s tomb in the afterlife |\n| **Detail** | Every soldier has a **unique face** — no two are alike |\n| **Weapons** | Soldiers held real weapons: swords, crossbows, spears |\n| **Organisation** | Arranged in battle formation — infantry, archers, cavalry, and charioteers |\n| **Colours** | Originally painted in bright colours, which faded after exposure to air |\n\n### Why It Matters\n\n- It shows the **power and wealth** of the first emperor\n- It reveals ancient Chinese beliefs about the **afterlife** (similar to Egypt — rulers wanted protection after death)\n- It is a **UNESCO World Heritage Site** and one of the greatest archaeological finds ever\n- It demonstrates the incredible **skill** of ancient Chinese craftspeople'),
  fb(8, 'The Terracotta Army was discovered in ___ by farmers digging a well. It contained over ___ life-sized clay soldiers.',
    ['1974', '8,000'],
    'The Terracotta Army was one of the greatest archaeological discoveries of the 20th century. Found near Xi\'an in 1974, it contained over 8,000 life-sized terracotta warriors, each with a unique face, arranged in battle formation to protect Emperor Qin Shi Huang in the afterlife.'),
  q(9, 'What do the Terracotta Army and the Egyptian pyramids have in common?',
    ['Both were built to protect powerful rulers in the afterlife — showing that different civilisations shared beliefs about life after death', 'Both were built by the same people', 'Both are located in China', 'Both were destroyed in wars'], 0,
    'Both ancient China and ancient Egypt believed in an afterlife and went to great lengths to prepare their rulers for it. The Egyptians built massive pyramids filled with treasures for the pharaoh\'s afterlife, while Qin Shi Huang had an entire clay army made to guard him in the next world. These parallels show common human concerns across very different civilisations.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Chinese Inventions ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## China\'s Four Great Inventions\n\nAncient China produced inventions that **changed the world**. The four most important are known as China\'s **"Four Great Inventions"**:\n\n### 1. Paper (about 105 CE)\n\n- Invented by **Cai Lun** during the **Han Dynasty**\n- Made from a mixture of tree bark, rags, old fishing nets, and hemp\n- Before paper, the Chinese wrote on bamboo strips (heavy) or silk (expensive)\n- Paper spread westward along the Silk Road, eventually reaching Europe\n- Paper made it possible to **record knowledge cheaply** and share it widely\n\n### 2. Printing (about 868 CE for woodblock; 1040 CE for movable type)\n\n- **Woodblock printing**: A whole page was carved into a wooden block, inked, and pressed onto paper\n- **Movable type**: Invented by **Bi Sheng** — individual characters were made from clay and could be rearranged to print different pages\n- Printing allowed books, official documents, and paper money to be produced much faster\n- This invention reached Europe centuries later and led to **Gutenberg\'s printing press** (1440s)'),
  q(2, 'Why was the invention of paper so important for the world?',
    ['It made it possible to record and share knowledge cheaply and easily, replacing expensive silk and heavy bamboo', 'It was only used for wrapping gifts', 'It was used to build houses in China', 'It had no impact outside of China'], 0,
    'Before paper, writing materials were either expensive (silk) or impractical (bamboo strips were heavy and bulky). Paper was cheap, light, and could be produced in large quantities. It revolutionised the recording and sharing of knowledge, eventually spreading from China along the Silk Road to the rest of the world.'),
  t(3, '## China\'s Four Great Inventions (continued)\n\n### 3. Gunpowder (about 9th century CE)\n\n- Discovered **accidentally** by Chinese alchemists who were trying to create a potion for eternal life\n- Made from a mixture of **saltpetre (potassium nitrate), sulphur, and charcoal**\n- First used for **fireworks** and **firecrackers** (to scare away evil spirits)\n- Later used for **weapons**: fire arrows, bombs, rockets, and early cannons\n- Spread to the Middle East and then to Europe, where it **transformed warfare** forever\n\n### 4. The Compass (about 11th century CE for navigation)\n\n- The Chinese discovered that a **magnetised needle** always points north\n- Originally used for **feng shui** (arranging buildings and rooms in harmony with nature)\n- Later used for **navigation at sea**, making long ocean voyages possible\n- Spread to Europe, where it helped European explorers navigate the oceans during the Age of Exploration\n\n### Other Chinese Inventions\n\n| Invention | Approximate Date |\n|-----------|------------------|\n| **Silk** | About 3000 BCE |\n| **Porcelain (china)** | About 600 CE |\n| **Wheelbarrow** | About 100 CE |\n| **Kite** | About 500 BCE |\n| **Umbrella** | About 1000 BCE |\n| **Seismograph** (earthquake detector) | About 132 CE |'),
  fb(4, 'Gunpowder was discovered accidentally by alchemists looking for a potion for ___. The ___ was originally used for feng shui before it was used for navigation.',
    ['eternal life', 'compass'],
    'Chinese alchemists were experimenting with different substances trying to find an "elixir of immortality" when they accidentally created gunpowder. The compass was first used in feng shui — the ancient Chinese practice of arranging objects and buildings to harmonise with the natural world. Only later was it adapted for navigation at sea.'),
  t(5, '## Daily Life in Ancient China\n\n### Family Life\n\nFamily was the **most important unit** in Chinese society. Key values included:\n\n- **Respect for elders** — children obeyed their parents, and younger people respected older people\n- **Ancestor worship** — the Chinese honoured their dead ancestors with offerings and ceremonies\n- **Filial piety** — the duty of children to care for and obey their parents (a key teaching of Confucius)\n- The **father** was the head of the household\n\n### Social Classes\n\n| Class | Who |\n|-------|-----|\n| **Emperor** | The "Son of Heaven" — supreme ruler |\n| **Nobles and officials** | Government administrators, chosen by examination |\n| **Farmers** | The majority of the population; respected because they produced food |\n| **Artisans** | Skilled craftspeople who made goods |\n| **Merchants** | Traders — considered the lowest class because they did not produce anything |\n\n### Food and Farming\n\n- **Rice** was the main crop in southern China; **wheat and millet** in the north\n- The Chinese invented **chopsticks** and developed techniques for **stir-frying** and **steaming**\n- **Tea** was first drunk in China and became central to Chinese culture\n- Farmers used **terracing** on hillsides to grow rice in paddy fields'),
  q(6, 'In ancient Chinese society, why were merchants considered the lowest social class?',
    ['Because they did not produce anything themselves — they only bought and sold goods made by others', 'Because they were all foreigners', 'Because they were the wealthiest people', 'Because the emperor said so without any reason'], 0,
    'Chinese philosophy, especially Confucianism, valued people who produced things. Farmers grew food, artisans made useful objects, but merchants only moved goods around for profit without creating anything new. Despite this low status, many merchants became very wealthy, especially those involved in Silk Road trade.'),
  t(7, '## Confucius and Chinese Philosophy\n\n**Confucius** (Kong Fuzi) lived from about **551 to 479 BCE** and is one of the most influential thinkers in history.\n\n### Key Teachings of Confucius\n\n| Principle | Meaning |\n|-----------|----------|\n| **Filial piety** | Children must respect and obey their parents |\n| **Respect for elders** | Younger people should honour and learn from older people |\n| **Education** | Learning and self-improvement are the most important goals in life |\n| **Good government** | Leaders should lead by moral example, not by force |\n| **The Golden Rule** | "Do not do to others what you would not want done to yourself" |\n| **Harmony** | Society works best when everyone fulfils their role with respect and kindness |\n\n### Impact\n\n- Confucius\' ideas shaped Chinese society for over **2,500 years**\n- The **civil service examination** system was based on Confucian ideas — government officials had to pass difficult exams rather than being born into their positions\n- Confucianism spread to **Korea, Japan, and Vietnam**\n- His teachings are still studied and followed today'),
  fb(8, 'The Chinese philosopher ___ taught that children must respect and obey their parents, a principle called ___.',
    ['Confucius', 'filial piety'],
    'Confucius (Kong Fuzi) is one of the most influential philosophers in history. Filial piety — the duty of children to respect, obey, and care for their parents — was at the heart of his teachings. This value shaped Chinese family life and society for thousands of years and is still important in Chinese culture today.'),
  q(9, 'Which of these statements best describes Confucius\' "Golden Rule"?',
    ['"Do not do to others what you would not want done to yourself" — treat people with the same respect you want to receive', '"The strongest person should rule over everyone"', '"Only educated people deserve rights"', '"Children should never speak to adults"'], 0,
    'Confucius\' Golden Rule is a principle of empathy and reciprocity: treat others the way you would want to be treated. Remarkably, similar ideas appear in many cultures and religions around the world. This shows that core moral principles about fairness and kindness are shared across human civilisations.'),
  t(10, '## Comparing Ancient Civilisations\n\nNow that we have studied ancient Egypt and ancient China, we can compare them:\n\n| Feature | Ancient Egypt | Ancient China |\n|---------|--------------|---------------|\n| **Location** | North-east Africa (Nile River) | East Asia (Huang He and Yangtze Rivers) |\n| **Writing** | Hieroglyphics | Chinese characters |\n| **Leaders** | Pharaohs (god-kings) | Emperors ("Son of Heaven") |\n| **Great structures** | Pyramids, temples | Great Wall, Terracotta Army |\n| **Key inventions** | Papyrus, calendar, medicine | Paper, printing, compass, gunpowder |\n| **Religion** | Many gods (polytheistic) | Ancestor worship, Confucianism, Taoism, later Buddhism |\n| **Afterlife beliefs** | Mummification, Book of the Dead | Terracotta Army, ancestor worship |\n| **Trade** | Traded along the Nile and with neighbouring regions | Silk Road connected China to the Mediterranean |\n\n### What They Had in Common\n\n- Both grew along **great rivers** that provided fertile soil\n- Both were ruled by **powerful rulers** believed to have divine authority\n- Both had **complex writing systems** and valued **education**\n- Both built **incredible monuments** that still amaze us today\n- Both believed in an **afterlife** and prepared their dead for the next world'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 4 (History – Term 2): Navigation and Exploration
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Early Navigation and Why People Explored ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Why Did People Explore?\n\nFor thousands of years, people have been driven to **explore** the world beyond their homes. But why?\n\n### Reasons for Exploration\n\n| Reason | Explanation |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Trade** | Finding new routes to buy and sell goods, especially spices, silk, and gold |\n| **Wealth** | Discovering new sources of gold, silver, and other riches |\n| **Religion** | Spreading Christianity (or Islam) to new lands |\n| **Power** | Countries wanted to build empires and control more territory |\n| **Curiosity** | A desire to learn about unknown lands and peoples |\n| **Competition** | Rival countries (especially Portugal and Spain) raced to claim new lands |\n\n### The Spice Trade\n\nOne of the biggest motivations was **spices**. In medieval Europe, spices like **pepper, cinnamon, cloves, and nutmeg** were incredibly valuable because:\n- They preserved food in an age before refrigerators\n- They flavoured bland food\n- They were used in medicines\n- They came from Asia and were very expensive by the time they reached Europe through middlemen\n\nEuropean countries wanted to find a **direct sea route to Asia** to buy spices cheaply, cutting out the Arab and Venetian middlemen who controlled the overland routes.'),
  q(2, 'Why were European countries so eager to find a sea route to Asia?',
    ['To buy valuable spices directly and cheaply, without having to go through expensive Arab and Venetian middlemen', 'Because they wanted to holiday in Asia', 'Because all the roads in Europe were broken', 'Because they had run out of water in Europe'], 0,
    'Spices from Asia (pepper, cinnamon, cloves, nutmeg) were enormously valuable in Europe for preserving and flavouring food and making medicines. But the overland trade routes were controlled by Arab and Venetian merchants who charged very high prices. European countries, especially Portugal and Spain, wanted to find their own sea routes to Asia to trade directly and keep the profits.'),
  t(3, '## How Did Early Navigators Find Their Way?\n\nBefore GPS, satellites, and modern maps, navigators used **natural signs** and simple **instruments** to find their way.\n\n### Navigation Methods\n\n| Method | How It Worked |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **Stars** | Sailors used the **North Star** (Polaris) and the **Southern Cross** to determine direction and latitude |\n| **Sun** | The sun rises in the east and sets in the west; its height at midday indicates latitude |\n| **Landmarks** | Mountains, islands, and coastlines helped sailors know where they were |\n| **Ocean currents and winds** | Experienced sailors knew the patterns of winds and currents |\n| **Bird watching** | Land birds flying meant land was nearby |\n| **Clouds** | Certain cloud formations indicated nearby land |\n\n### Navigation Instruments\n\n| Instrument | Purpose |\n|------------|----------|\n| **Magnetic compass** | A magnetised needle that always points north — originally invented in China |\n| **Astrolabe** | Measured the angle of the sun or stars above the horizon to calculate latitude |\n| **Cross-staff** | A simpler tool to measure the height of the North Star or sun |\n| **Quadrant** | Similar to an astrolabe — measured the angle of Polaris to find latitude |\n| **Portolan charts** | Early sea maps showing coastlines, ports, and compass directions |\n| **Hourglass** | Measured time at sea |'),
  fb(4, 'Sailors used the ___ Star to find north, and the Southern ___ to navigate in the southern hemisphere.',
    ['North', 'Cross'],
    'The North Star (Polaris) appears almost directly above the North Pole and has been used for navigation for centuries. In the southern hemisphere, where Polaris cannot be seen, sailors used the Southern Cross constellation to find south. These celestial navigation methods were essential before the magnetic compass became widely available.'),
  t(5, '## Portugal — Leader of Exploration\n\n**Portugal** was a small country on the western edge of Europe, but it became the **leader of sea exploration** in the 1400s.\n\n### Prince Henry the Navigator\n\n**Prince Henry** (1394–1460) was a Portuguese prince who is often called the "father of exploration." He:\n- Set up a **navigation school** at Sagres in southern Portugal\n- Funded expeditions to explore the **west coast of Africa**\n- Encouraged the development of better **ships** (the **caravel** — a small, fast ship that could sail against the wind)\n- Improved **maps and navigation instruments**\n- His goal: to find a sea route **around Africa to Asia**\n\n### Portuguese Exploration Down the African Coast\n\n| Year | Achievement |\n|------|------------|\n| **1419** | Portuguese sailors reached **Madeira** |\n| **1427** | Discovered the **Azores** |\n| **1434** | Gil Eanes sailed past **Cape Bojador** (a feared point on the African coast) |\n| **1460s–1480s** | Portuguese traders established posts along the **West African coast**, trading in gold, ivory, and enslaved people |\n| **1488** | **Bartolomeu Dias** rounded the southern tip of Africa |\n| **1498** | **Vasco da Gama** reached India by sailing around Africa |\n\nPortugal\'s exploration of the African coast was motivated by trade, but it also led to the **exploitation and colonisation** of African peoples and lands.'),
  q(6, 'Why is Prince Henry of Portugal called "the Navigator"?',
    ['Because he funded and organised expeditions, built a navigation school, and drove Portuguese exploration of the African coast', 'Because he personally sailed around the world', 'Because he invented the compass', 'Because he drew the first map of Portugal'], 0,
    'Prince Henry never sailed on the expeditions himself, but he was the driving force behind Portuguese exploration. He set up a navigation school, funded expeditions, improved ships and instruments, and sent sailors further and further down the African coast. His vision and investment made Portugal the leading exploration nation.'),
  t(7, '## Bartolomeu Dias — Rounding the Cape\n\nIn **1488**, the Portuguese explorer **Bartolomeu Dias** became the first European to sail **around the southern tip of Africa**.\n\n### The Voyage\n\n| Detail | Fact |\n|--------|------|\n| **Left Lisbon** | August 1487 |\n| **Ships** | Three small caravels |\n| **Route** | Down the west coast of Africa, past the Cape |\n| **The storm** | A fierce storm blew his ships south and then east — when it cleared, they were past the Cape without seeing it |\n| **Turned back** | His crew was exhausted and afraid; they forced Dias to turn back |\n| **Saw the Cape** | On the return voyage, Dias finally saw the southern tip of Africa |\n| **Named it** | Dias called it the **"Cape of Storms"**, but King John II of Portugal renamed it the **"Cape of Good Hope"** because it gave hope of reaching India |\n| **Returned** | December 1488 |\n\n### Significance for South Africa\n\nDias\' voyage was a turning point for southern Africa:\n- It proved that ships could sail **around Africa** to reach the Indian Ocean\n- It opened the way for **Vasco da Gama** to reach India ten years later\n- It began European interest in southern Africa that would eventually lead to **colonisation**\n- The Khoikhoi at the Cape had their first encounters with European sailors'),
  fb(8, 'Bartolomeu Dias sailed around the southern tip of Africa in ___. He originally named it the "Cape of ___".',
    ['1488', 'Storms'],
    'Dias experienced terrible storms that actually blew his ships around the Cape without him seeing it. He named the point Cabo das Tormentas (Cape of Storms), but King John II of Portugal renamed it Cabo da Boa Esperan\u00e7a (Cape of Good Hope) because it gave hope of finding a sea route to the lucrative spice trade in India.'),
  q(9, 'Why did King John II rename the "Cape of Storms" to the "Cape of Good Hope"?',
    ['Because rounding the Cape gave hope that a sea route to the riches of India had been found', 'Because the weather there was always good', 'Because he hoped Dias would never come back', 'Because the local people asked him to change the name'], 0,
    'The king understood the enormous significance of Dias\' achievement: if ships could round the southern tip of Africa, they could reach India and the spice trade by sea. The "Cape of Good Hope" expressed his hope that this route would bring great wealth to Portugal — which it did when Vasco da Gama reached India in 1498.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Vasco da Gama and the Impact on Africa ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Vasco da Gama — The Sea Route to India\n\nTen years after Dias rounded the Cape, **Vasco da Gama** completed the journey to **India**.\n\n### The Voyage\n\n| Detail | Fact |\n|--------|------|\n| **Left Lisbon** | 8 July 1497 |\n| **Ships** | Four ships and about 170 crew |\n| **Route** | Down the Atlantic, around the Cape of Good Hope, up the east coast of Africa, across the Indian Ocean to India |\n| **Stops in Africa** | Mossel Bay, Natal (he named it — arrived on Christmas Day / Natal in Portuguese), Mozambique, Mombasa, Malindi |\n| **Guide** | An experienced Arab navigator, **Ahmad ibn Majid**, helped guide da Gama across the Indian Ocean from Malindi |\n| **Arrived in India** | **20 May 1498** — reached Calicut (now Kozhikode) on the south-west coast of India |\n| **Returned to Lisbon** | September 1499, with only two ships and about 55 surviving crew |\n\n### What He Found in India\n\n- A wealthy trading port with merchants from Arabia, India, China, and Southeast Asia\n- Spices (pepper, cinnamon, cloves, ginger), precious stones, and silk\n- The local ruler (Zamorin) was not impressed by the cheap goods the Portuguese offered in trade\n- Da Gama returned to Portugal with enough spices to pay for the expedition **60 times over**'),
  q(2, 'How did Vasco da Gama successfully cross the Indian Ocean from Africa to India?',
    ['An experienced Arab navigator, Ahmad ibn Majid, guided him from Malindi using knowledge of monsoon winds', 'He used a GPS device', 'He followed a map drawn by Bartolomeu Dias', 'He flew across in a hot air balloon'], 0,
    'Da Gama relied on the expertise of Ahmad ibn Majid, an Arab navigator who knew the Indian Ocean\'s monsoon wind patterns. Arab and Swahili traders had been sailing these routes for centuries. This highlights that African and Asian sailors had sophisticated navigation knowledge long before the Europeans arrived.'),
  t(3, '## The East African Coast Before the Europeans\n\nBefore the Portuguese arrived, the **east coast of Africa** was home to wealthy, advanced **Swahili trading cities**.\n\n### Swahili Trading Cities\n\n| City | Location | What Was Traded |\n|------|----------|----------------|\n| **Kilwa** | Tanzania | Gold (from Great Zimbabwe and the interior), ivory, iron |\n| **Mombasa** | Kenya | Ivory, spices, enslaved people |\n| **Malindi** | Kenya | Trade hub with links to India and Arabia |\n| **Sofala** | Mozambique | Gold from the Zimbabwe plateau |\n| **Mogadishu** | Somalia | Cloth, gold, ivory |\n\n### Key Facts About Swahili Coast Trade\n\n- African, Arab, Indian, and even Chinese merchants traded here for **centuries** before Europeans arrived\n- The Swahili people developed their own language (Kiswahili — a Bantu language with Arabic influences) and culture\n- They built **stone buildings** (including the famous ruins of Kilwa and the Great Mosque)\n- They traded **gold, ivory, iron, enslaved people, and animal skins** for **cloth, beads, porcelain, and spices**\n- Chinese **porcelain** and coins have been found at archaeological sites along the Swahili coast, proving long-distance trade\n\nThe Portuguese arrival disrupted this thriving trade network.'),
  fb(4, 'The wealthy trading cities on the east coast of Africa were known as ___ cities. Gold from the interior of Africa was exported through the port of ___.',
    ['Swahili', 'Sofala'],
    'The Swahili coast was a thriving network of trading cities built by Bantu-speaking Africans who traded with Arab, Indian, and Chinese merchants. Sofala (in modern Mozambique) was the main port through which gold from the Zimbabwe plateau reached the Indian Ocean trade network. This trade made the Swahili cities wealthy and culturally rich.'),
  t(5, '## The Impact of European Exploration on Africa\n\nEuropean exploration had **devastating consequences** for Africa and its people.\n\n### Immediate Effects\n\n| Impact | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Destruction of Swahili cities** | The Portuguese attacked and destroyed cities like Kilwa and Mombasa to control trade |\n| **Loss of trade control** | Africans lost control of their own trade routes as Europeans took over |\n| **Introduction of diseases** | Europeans brought diseases (smallpox, measles) that killed many Africans |\n| **Violence** | The Portuguese used superior weapons (cannons) to force African and Asian rulers to submit |\n\n### The Slave Trade\n\n- The Portuguese were the first Europeans to begin the **Atlantic slave trade**\n- They captured and bought enslaved Africans along the West African coast\n- Millions of Africans were taken across the Atlantic to work on plantations in the Americas\n- This continued for over **300 years** and was one of the greatest crimes in human history\n\n### Colonisation\n\n- European exploration eventually led to the **colonisation** of almost all of Africa\n- By the late 1800s, European countries had divided Africa among themselves at the **Berlin Conference (1884-1885)** — without any African input\n- Colonisation meant the loss of African **land, resources, freedom, and culture**'),
  q(6, 'How did the Portuguese gain control of trade along the east African coast?',
    ['They used superior weapons like cannons to attack and destroy Swahili trading cities such as Kilwa and Mombasa', 'They traded fairly and were welcomed by everyone', 'They built better ships than the Swahili people', 'They were invited by the Swahili leaders to take over'], 0,
    'The Portuguese used military force — particularly their cannons, which were more powerful than anything the Swahili cities had — to attack and destroy cities like Kilwa and Mombasa. They then built forts (like Fort Jesus in Mombasa) to maintain control. This violent takeover disrupted centuries of peaceful and profitable Indian Ocean trade.'),
  t(7, '## Exploration and South Africa\n\nEuropean exploration directly affected the southern tip of Africa.\n\n### Timeline of European Contact with Southern Africa\n\n| Date | Event |\n|------|-------|\n| **1488** | Bartolomeu Dias rounds the Cape of Good Hope |\n| **1497** | Vasco da Gama stops at Mossel Bay and names "Natal" |\n| **1500s–1600s** | European ships stop at the Cape for fresh water and food, trading with the Khoikhoi |\n| **1652** | The Dutch East India Company (VOC) establishes a permanent settlement at the Cape under **Jan van Riebeeck** |\n| **1659–1677** | Khoikhoi-Dutch wars as settlers take Khoikhoi land |\n| **1795** | The British take control of the Cape Colony |\n\n### The Connection to South Africa Today\n\n- The European "discovery" of the sea route around Africa set in motion events that would lead to:\n  - The **colonisation** of South Africa\n  - The **dispossession** of indigenous peoples (San, Khoikhoi, and Bantu-speaking groups)\n  - **Slavery** at the Cape\n  - Eventually, **apartheid** (1948–1994)\n\n- But it also led to South Africa becoming the **diverse, multicultural nation** it is today\n- Understanding this history helps us understand why South Africa looks the way it does today'),
  fb(8, 'In 1652, the Dutch East India Company established a settlement at the Cape under ___. This was the beginning of European ___ in South Africa.',
    ['Jan van Riebeeck', 'colonisation'],
    'Jan van Riebeeck arrived at the Cape in 1652 to set up a refreshment station for Dutch ships travelling to and from Asia. What started as a small supply point grew into a full colony. This was the beginning of European colonisation of South Africa, which would last for over 300 years and have profound consequences for all the people of the region.'),
  q(9, 'Why is it important for South Africans to study the history of European exploration?',
    ['Because European exploration led directly to the colonisation of South Africa and the events that shaped the country we live in today', 'Because it is only about European achievements', 'Because it has nothing to do with Africa', 'Because only Europeans can learn from this history'], 0,
    'The European exploration of Africa\'s coasts in the 1400s and 1500s set in motion a chain of events — colonisation, slavery, land dispossession, and eventually apartheid — that profoundly shaped South Africa. Understanding this history helps us understand why South Africa is the way it is today and why issues of land, inequality, and reconciliation are still important.'),
  t(10, '## Summary: Navigation and Exploration\n\n### Key Points\n\n- European exploration was driven by the desire for **trade, wealth, power, and religion**\n- Early navigators used **stars, compasses, astrolabes**, and other instruments to find their way\n- **Portugal** led the way, with Prince Henry funding exploration down the African coast\n- **Bartolomeu Dias** rounded the Cape of Good Hope in **1488**\n- **Vasco da Gama** reached India in **1498**, opening the sea route from Europe to Asia\n- The east African coast had **thriving Swahili trading cities** long before Europeans arrived\n- European exploration led to the **destruction of African trade networks, the slave trade, and colonisation**\n- These events directly shaped the history of **South Africa**\n\n### Critical Thinking\n\nExploration is often presented as a story of brave European adventurers "discovering" new lands. But we must always ask:\n- Who was **already living** in these "discovered" lands?\n- What happened to the **local people** when the explorers arrived?\n- Whose story is being told — and whose story is **missing**?'),
];

