// CHAPTER 5 (Geography – Term 3): Maps and Map Skills
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Types of Maps and Compass Directions ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is a Map?\n\nA **map** is a flat drawing or picture of the Earth\'s surface, or part of it, seen from **above** (a bird\'s eye view).\n\nMaps are useful because:\n- They show us where places are\n- They help us find our way (navigate)\n- They show information about the land, weather, population, and more\n- They can be carried easily (unlike a globe)\n\n### Maps vs Globes\n\n| Feature | Map | Globe |\n|---------|-----|-------|\n| **Shape** | Flat (2D) | Round (3D) |\n| **Accuracy** | Some distortion (stretching) because a round earth is shown on a flat surface | More accurate — same shape as Earth |\n| **Portability** | Easy to fold and carry | Heavy and bulky |\n| **Detail** | Can show a small area in great detail | Shows the whole world but less detail |\n| **Uses** | Road navigation, classroom study, hiking | Understanding Earth\'s true shape and size |'),
  q(2, 'Why is there some distortion on flat maps?',
    ['Because a round Earth is being shown on a flat surface, so shapes and sizes get stretched', 'Because mapmakers are not careful', 'Because the Earth is actually flat', 'Because maps are always wrong'], 0,
    'The Earth is a sphere (round), but maps are flat. Imagine peeling an orange and trying to press the peel flat — it would stretch and tear. The same problem occurs with maps: areas near the poles often look bigger than they really are (e.g., Greenland looks huge on many maps but is actually much smaller than Africa).'),
  t(3, '## Compass Directions\n\nTo read a map, you need to know **compass directions**.\n\n### The Four Cardinal Directions\n\n| Direction | Abbreviation | Position |\n|-----------|-------------|----------|\n| **North** | N | Top of most maps |\n| **South** | S | Bottom |\n| **East** | E | Right |\n| **West** | W | Left |\n\nA helpful way to remember: **N**ever **E**at **S**limy **W**orms (going clockwise from North).\n\n### Intercardinal (In-Between) Directions\n\n| Direction | Abbreviation | Between |\n|-----------|-------------|----------|\n| **North-East** | NE | North and East |\n| **South-East** | SE | South and East |\n| **South-West** | SW | South and West |\n| **North-West** | NW | North and West |\n\n### Using a Compass\n\nA **compass** is an instrument with a magnetised needle that always points to **magnetic north**. On a map, the **north arrow** (or compass rose) shows which direction is north. Once you know where north is, you can find all the other directions.\n\n### South African Examples\n\n- Cape Town is in the **south-west** of South Africa\n- Durban is on the **east** coast\n- Musina (Limpopo) is in the **north**\n- The Indian Ocean is to the **east** and **south-east** of South Africa\n- The Atlantic Ocean is to the **west** and **south-west**'),
  fb(4, 'The four main compass directions are called the ___ directions. On most maps, north is at the ___.',
    ['cardinal', 'top'],
    'The four cardinal directions are North, South, East, and West. By convention, most maps are drawn with north at the top. The north arrow or compass rose on a map always shows you which direction is north. Once you know where north is, you can work out all other directions.'),
  t(5, '## Types of Maps\n\nDifferent maps show different information.\n\n### Common Types of Maps\n\n| Type | What It Shows | Example |\n|------|--------------|----------|\n| **Physical map** | Natural features: mountains, rivers, lakes, oceans, deserts | A map showing the Drakensberg mountains and the Orange River |\n| **Political map** | Human-made boundaries: countries, provinces, cities, towns | A map showing South Africa\'s nine provinces |\n| **Topographic map** | Detailed features including roads, buildings, contour lines (showing height) | A 1:50 000 map of your local area |\n| **Road map** | Roads, highways, and distances between places | A map showing the N1 from Johannesburg to Cape Town |\n| **Climate/weather map** | Temperature, rainfall, and weather patterns | A map showing South Africa\'s climate zones |\n| **Population map** | Where people live and how many | A map showing population density in Gauteng |\n| **Resource map** | Where natural resources are found | A map showing gold mining areas in SA |\n\n### Parts of a Map (BOSS-D)\n\nEvery good map has these five features, remembered by the acronym **BOSS-D**:\n\n| Letter | Feature | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|----------|\n| **B** | **Border** | A frame or line around the edge of the map |\n| **O** | **Orientation** | A north arrow or compass rose showing direction |\n| **S** | **Scale** | Shows the relationship between distance on the map and distance in real life |\n| **S** | **Symbols/Key** | Pictures and colours that represent features (roads, rivers, buildings, etc.) |\n| **D** | **Description/Title** | The title tells you what area the map shows and what it is about |'),
  q(6, 'What does the acronym BOSS-D help you remember?',
    ['The five essential features of a good map: Border, Orientation, Scale, Symbols/Key, and Description/Title', 'The five oceans of the world', 'The five largest countries in Africa', 'The five provinces of South Africa'], 0,
    'BOSS-D is a useful acronym for remembering the five essential features every good map should have: Border (frame), Orientation (north arrow), Scale (distance relationship), Symbols/Key (legend explaining symbols), and Description/Title (what the map shows). When reading or drawing a map, always check for all five.'),
  t(7, '## Map Symbols and the Key (Legend)\n\nMaps use **symbols** — small pictures, colours, and patterns — to represent real features. The **key** (or legend) is a box on the map that explains what each symbol means.\n\n### Common Map Symbols\n\n| Symbol | Represents |\n|--------|------------|\n| Blue lines | Rivers and streams |\n| Blue areas | Dams, lakes, and oceans |\n| Green areas | Parks, forests, or vegetation |\n| Brown lines (contour lines) | Height above sea level (on topographic maps) |\n| Red or yellow lines | Main roads and highways |\n| Black dots | Towns and cities (bigger dot = bigger place) |\n| A star or circle | Capital city |\n| Crossed pickaxes | Mine |\n| An aeroplane | Airport |\n| A tent | Camping site |\n| A cross | Church |\n| H | Hospital |\n\n### Colours on Maps\n\n| Colour | Usually Represents |\n|--------|--------------------|\n| **Blue** | Water (rivers, lakes, oceans) |\n| **Green** | Vegetation, low-lying land, forests |\n| **Brown/orange** | Higher land, mountains, deserts |\n| **White** | Very high land, snow, or ice |\n| **Yellow** | Semi-arid land, savanna |\n| **Red/pink** | Roads, or densely populated areas on population maps |'),
  fb(8, 'The box on a map that explains what each symbol means is called the ___. Blue areas on a map usually represent ___.',
    ['key', 'water'],
    'The key (or legend) is essential for reading any map correctly. Without it, you would not know what the symbols and colours mean. Blue is the standard colour for water features on maps — rivers, lakes, dams, and oceans are all shown in blue.'),
  q(9, 'You are looking at a map and see a series of brown lines close together. What do these represent?',
    ['Contour lines showing steep terrain — the lines are close together because the land rises sharply', 'Rivers flowing close to each other', 'Fences around a farm', 'Roads in a busy area'], 0,
    'Contour lines on a topographic map connect points of equal height above sea level. When contour lines are close together, it means the land is steep (the height changes rapidly over a short distance). When they are far apart, the land is gentle or flat.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Scale and Grid References ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Understanding Map Scale\n\n**Scale** tells you the relationship between distance on a map and the actual distance on the ground.\n\n### Why Scale Matters\n\nA map is much smaller than the real area it shows. Scale tells you **how much smaller**. For example:\n- A scale of **1:50 000** means that 1 cm on the map equals 50,000 cm (or 500 m, or 0.5 km) in real life\n- A scale of **1:100 000** means 1 cm on the map equals 1 km in real life\n\n### Types of Scale\n\n| Type | What It Looks Like | Example |\n|------|-------------------|----------|\n| **Word scale** | Written in words | "1 cm represents 500 m" |\n| **Ratio scale** | Written as a ratio | 1:50 000 |\n| **Line (bar) scale** | A line on the map divided into sections showing real-life distances | A small ruler on the map marked 0, 1 km, 2 km, 3 km |\n\n### Large Scale vs Small Scale\n\n| Feature | Large-Scale Map | Small-Scale Map |\n|---------|----------------|------------------|\n| **Ratio** | 1:10 000 or 1:50 000 | 1:1 000 000 or smaller |\n| **Area shown** | Small area (your neighbourhood) | Large area (whole country or continent) |\n| **Detail** | Lots of detail (individual buildings, streets) | Less detail (only major cities, rivers) |\n| **Example** | A map of your school grounds | A map of Africa |'),
  q(2, 'On a map with a scale of 1:50 000, two towns are 4 cm apart. How far apart are they in real life?',
    ['2 km — because 1 cm = 500 m (or 0.5 km), so 4 cm = 2,000 m = 2 km', '4 km', '50 km', '200 m'], 0,
    'With a scale of 1:50 000, every 1 cm on the map represents 50,000 cm in real life. Convert: 50,000 cm = 500 m = 0.5 km. So 4 cm on the map = 4 x 0.5 km = 2 km in real life. Always multiply the map distance by the scale factor and convert to useful units.'),
  t(3, '## Grid References\n\nMaps often have a **grid** — a pattern of horizontal and vertical lines that divide the map into squares. Grid references help you find exact locations on a map.\n\n### How Grid References Work\n\n- Vertical lines are called **eastings** (they increase as you go east / to the right)\n- Horizontal lines are called **northings** (they increase as you go north / upward)\n- You always give the **easting first**, then the **northing**\n- Remember: **"Along the corridor and up the stairs"** — read across (easting) first, then up (northing)\n\n### Example\n\nIf a school is located in the square where easting 34 meets northing 57, the grid reference is **3457**.\n\n### Six-Figure Grid References\n\nFor a more precise location, we use **six-figure grid references**:\n\n1. Read the easting: **34**\n2. Estimate how far across the square (in tenths): say **7** tenths → **347**\n3. Read the northing: **57**\n4. Estimate how far up the square (in tenths): say **3** tenths → **573**\n5. Six-figure grid reference: **347573**\n\nThis pinpoints a much more exact location within the grid square.'),
  fb(4, 'Vertical lines on a grid are called ___ and they increase as you go east. You always read grid references along the corridor first, then up the ___.',
    ['eastings', 'stairs'],
    'Eastings are the vertical lines numbered from left to right (west to east). Northings are the horizontal lines numbered from bottom to top (south to north). The phrase "along the corridor and up the stairs" helps you remember: always give the easting (across) first, then the northing (up). This is similar to how you would navigate a building — walk along the corridor, then go up the stairs.'),
  t(5, '## Reading a Map of South Africa\n\nLet\'s apply our map skills to South Africa.\n\n### South Africa\'s Nine Provinces\n\n| Province | Capital | Location |\n|----------|---------|----------|\n| **Gauteng** | Johannesburg (largest city) / Pretoria (admin capital) | North-central |\n| **KwaZulu-Natal** | Pietermaritzburg | East coast |\n| **Western Cape** | Cape Town | South-west |\n| **Eastern Cape** | Bhisho | South-east |\n| **Free State** | Bloemfontein (judicial capital) | Central |\n| **Limpopo** | Polokwane | Far north |\n| **Mpumalanga** | Mbombela (Nelspruit) | North-east |\n| **North West** | Mahikeng | North-west |\n| **Northern Cape** | Kimberley | West-central (largest province by area) |\n\n### South Africa\'s Neighbours\n\n| Direction | Neighbouring Country |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| **North** | Zimbabwe, Botswana |\n| **North-east** | Mozambique |\n| **North-west** | Namibia |\n| **Surrounded by SA** | Lesotho (completely inside SA), Eswatini (partly inside) |\n\n### Bodies of Water\n\n- **Indian Ocean** — east and south-east coast\n- **Atlantic Ocean** — west coast\n- The two oceans meet near **Cape Agulhas** — the southernmost point of Africa (NOT the Cape of Good Hope!)'),
  q(6, 'Which is the southernmost point of Africa?',
    ['Cape Agulhas — not the Cape of Good Hope, which is a common misconception', 'The Cape of Good Hope', 'Durban', 'Cape Town'], 0,
    'Many people think the Cape of Good Hope is Africa\'s southernmost point, but it is actually Cape Agulhas, located about 150 km to the south-east. Cape Agulhas is also roughly where the Indian Ocean and the Atlantic Ocean meet. The Cape of Good Hope is the more famous landmark but not the most southern.'),
  t(7, '## Measuring Distance on a Map\n\n### Using the Line (Bar) Scale\n\nThe simplest way to measure distance on a map:\n\n1. Place a piece of paper or string along the route between two points\n2. Mark the start and end points on the paper\n3. Place the paper against the **bar scale** on the map\n4. Read off the real-life distance\n\n### For Straight-Line Distance\n\n1. Measure the distance between two points in **centimetres** using a ruler\n2. Multiply by the scale factor\n3. Convert to kilometres\n\n**Example**: Distance on map = 6 cm, Scale = 1:50 000\n- 6 cm x 50,000 = 300,000 cm\n- 300,000 cm \u00f7 100 = 3,000 m\n- 3,000 m \u00f7 1,000 = **3 km**\n\n### For Curved Routes (like a river or winding road)\n\n1. Place a piece of **string** along the curved route\n2. Mark the start and end on the string\n3. Straighten the string and measure with a ruler\n4. Apply the scale as above\n\n### South African Example\n\nOn a road map of South Africa with scale 1:1,000,000:\n- 1 cm on the map = 10 km in real life\n- If Johannesburg to Pretoria measures about 5.8 cm on the map, the real distance is about **58 km**'),
  fb(8, 'On a map with scale 1:50 000, one centimetre represents ___ metres in real life. The technique of using a piece of ___ helps measure curved distances on a map.',
    ['500', 'string'],
    'With a scale of 1:50 000, every centimetre on the map represents 50,000 cm in real life, which equals 500 m or 0.5 km. For curved routes like rivers or winding roads, a straight ruler does not work well, so you lay a piece of string along the curve, then straighten it to measure the total distance.'),
  q(9, 'On a 1:100 000 map, a road measures 8.5 cm. What is the real distance?',
    ['8.5 km — because 1 cm equals 1 km on a 1:100 000 map', '85 km', '850 m', '0.85 km'], 0,
    'On a 1:100 000 map, 1 cm = 100,000 cm = 1,000 m = 1 km. So 8.5 cm on the map = 8.5 km in real life. This is a useful scale to memorise: 1:100 000 means 1 cm = 1 km.'),
  t(10, '## Putting Your Map Skills Together\n\nGood map skills help you in many ways:\n\n- **Finding places** — using grid references and the key\n- **Measuring distances** — using the scale\n- **Describing direction** — using compass directions\n- **Understanding the landscape** — using contour lines, symbols, and colours\n- **Planning routes** — choosing the best path from one place to another\n\n### Tips for Map Reading\n\n1. Always check the **title** first — what area does the map show?\n2. Check the **key/legend** — what do the symbols mean?\n3. Find the **north arrow** — which way is north?\n4. Check the **scale** — how far is 1 cm in real life?\n5. Use **grid references** to pinpoint exact locations\n6. Use **compass directions** to describe where things are relative to each other\n\nMap skills are essential for geography, but they are also practical life skills. Whether you are using a paper map, Google Maps, or a GPS, the same principles of direction, scale, and symbols apply.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 6 (Geography – Term 3): Food and Farming in South Africa
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Types of Farming ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Why Is Farming Important?\n\n**Farming** (agriculture) is one of the most important activities in South Africa and the world. Without farming, there would be no food to eat.\n\n### Farming Provides\n\n| What | Examples |\n|------|----------|\n| **Food** | Maize, wheat, fruit, vegetables, meat, milk, eggs |\n| **Raw materials** | Cotton (for clothes), wool, leather, sugar cane, timber |\n| **Jobs** | Farming employs about **5% of South Africans** directly, and many more in related industries (food processing, transport, retail) |\n| **Export income** | South Africa exports fruit, wine, maize, sugar, and wool, earning valuable foreign currency |\n\n### Types of Farming\n\nThere are several ways to classify farming:\n\n| Type | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Crop farming (arable)** | Growing plants — maize, wheat, fruit, vegetables, sugar cane |\n| **Livestock farming (pastoral)** | Raising animals — cattle, sheep, goats, chickens, pigs |\n| **Mixed farming** | Both crops and livestock on the same farm |\n| **Subsistence farming** | Farming to feed your own family — any surplus may be sold or traded |\n| **Commercial farming** | Large-scale farming to sell products for profit |'),
  q(2, 'What is the main difference between subsistence farming and commercial farming?',
    ['Subsistence farming produces food mainly to feed the farmer\'s own family, while commercial farming produces food to sell for profit', 'Subsistence farming only grows maize, while commercial farming only raises cattle', 'Commercial farming is done in cities, subsistence farming is done in the ocean', 'There is no difference between them'], 0,
    'Subsistence farmers grow crops or raise animals primarily to feed themselves and their families. Any surplus might be sold at local markets for extra income. Commercial farmers, on the other hand, farm on a large scale specifically to sell their products for profit. Commercial farms typically use modern machinery, fertilisers, and irrigation systems.'),
  t(3, '## Farming Regions of South Africa\n\nSouth Africa has diverse climates and landscapes, which means different areas are suited to different types of farming.\n\n### Major Farming Regions\n\n| Region | What Is Farmed | Why This Area? |\n|--------|---------------|----------------|\n| **Mpumalanga Lowveld** | Sugar cane, citrus, tropical fruit, macadamia nuts | Warm, subtropical climate with good rainfall |\n| **Western Cape** | Wine grapes, deciduous fruit (apples, pears, plums), wheat | Mediterranean climate — wet winters, dry summers |\n| **Free State** | Maize, wheat, sunflowers | Flat grasslands, moderate rainfall, fertile soil |\n| **KwaZulu-Natal coast** | Sugar cane, bananas, subtropical fruit | Warm, humid climate |\n| **Limpopo** | Tomatoes, potatoes, citrus, avocados, game farming | Warm climate, irrigation from rivers |\n| **Eastern Cape and Karoo** | Sheep (wool and meat), goats (mohair) | Semi-arid grassland ideal for grazing |\n| **Gauteng and surrounds** | Maize, vegetables, dairy, poultry | Close to the biggest market (Johannesburg) |\n| **Northern Cape** | Grapes (raisins and wine), wheat (irrigation farming along the Orange River) | Hot, dry — irrigation-dependent |\n\n### The Maize Triangle\n\nThe **Maize Triangle** is South Africa\'s most important crop-farming region:\n- Located in the **Free State, North West, and Mpumalanga** provinces\n- Produces most of South Africa\'s **maize** (mealies) — the country\'s staple food\n- Flat land, good soils, and adequate summer rainfall make it ideal for maize'),
  fb(4, 'The ___ is a major maize-producing region located in the Free State, North West, and Mpumalanga. The Western Cape is famous for farming wine grapes because it has a ___ climate.',
    ['Maize Triangle', 'Mediterranean'],
    'The Maize Triangle is South Africa\'s breadbasket — it produces most of the country\'s maize, which is the staple food for millions of South Africans. The Western Cape has a Mediterranean climate (wet, cool winters and hot, dry summers), which is ideal for growing wine grapes, apples, pears, and wheat.'),
  t(5, '## Subsistence Farming in South Africa\n\nMillions of South Africans depend on **subsistence farming** to survive.\n\n### Where Does Subsistence Farming Happen?\n\n- Mainly in **rural areas** of the former homelands (Transkei, Venda, KwaZulu, etc.)\n- In the **Eastern Cape, KwaZulu-Natal, Limpopo**, and other rural provinces\n\n### Characteristics of Subsistence Farming\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Scale** | Small plots of land (often less than 1 hectare) |\n| **Labour** | Family members do the work by hand |\n| **Tools** | Simple tools — hoes, hand ploughs |\n| **Crops** | Maize, beans, pumpkins, sweet potatoes, spinach |\n| **Animals** | A few chickens, goats, or cattle |\n| **Output** | Mostly for the family to eat; small amounts may be sold |\n| **Challenges** | Poor soil, unreliable rainfall, no irrigation, no fertiliser, pests |\n\n### Challenges Facing Subsistence Farmers\n\n- **Lack of water** — many areas have no irrigation and depend on rain\n- **Poor soil** — years of farming without fertiliser has depleted the soil\n- **Small land size** — apartheid land policies left many black South Africans with tiny plots\n- **Climate change** — more droughts and unpredictable rainfall\n- **Lack of support** — limited access to loans, training, seeds, and markets'),
  q(6, 'Why are former homeland areas in South Africa often associated with subsistence farming?',
    ['Because apartheid policies confined many black South Africans to small, often less fertile areas, leaving them with limited resources for large-scale farming', 'Because the land there is the most fertile in South Africa', 'Because people in those areas prefer subsistence farming', 'Because commercial farming was banned in those areas after 1994'], 0,
    'Under apartheid, the homeland system (Bantustans) confined the majority of South Africa\'s black population to just 13% of the land. These areas were often overcrowded, had poor soil, and lacked infrastructure. This legacy means many people in former homeland areas still practice subsistence farming on small, under-resourced plots.'),
  t(7, '## Irrigation Farming\n\nMuch of South Africa is **semi-arid** (dry). In these areas, farming is only possible with **irrigation** — the artificial supply of water to crops.\n\n### Major Irrigation Schemes\n\n| Scheme | Location | Crops |\n|--------|----------|-------|\n| **Vaalharts** | Northern Cape / North West | Wheat, maize, groundnuts, cotton |\n| **Orange River (Gariep Dam)** | Northern Cape | Grapes, wheat, lucerne |\n| **Loskop Dam** | Mpumalanga / Limpopo | Citrus, tobacco, wheat |\n| **Breede River** | Western Cape | Wine grapes, deciduous fruit |\n\n### Methods of Irrigation\n\n| Method | How It Works | Pros/Cons |\n|--------|-------------|------------|\n| **Flood irrigation** | Water floods across flat fields | Simple but wastes water |\n| **Sprinkler** | Water sprayed from overhead sprinklers | Good coverage but water lost to evaporation |\n| **Drip irrigation** | Water dripped slowly directly to plant roots through tubes | Very efficient — uses the least water |\n| **Centre pivot** | Large rotating sprinklers create circular crop patterns | Efficient for large areas |\n\nSouth Africa is a **water-scarce country**, so farmers increasingly use **drip irrigation** to save water.'),
  fb(8, 'South Africa is a ___-scarce country, so farmers must irrigate wisely. The most water-efficient method of irrigation is ___.',
    ['water', 'drip irrigation'],
    'South Africa receives an average annual rainfall of about 464 mm — well below the world average of 860 mm. This makes South Africa a water-scarce country. Drip irrigation is the most efficient method because it delivers water directly to plant roots through tubes, minimising evaporation and waste. It can save up to 60% more water than flood irrigation.'),
  q(9, 'Why is the Maize Triangle located in the Free State, North West, and Mpumalanga rather than in the Northern Cape?',
    ['Because the Maize Triangle receives adequate summer rainfall, while the Northern Cape is too dry and would require expensive irrigation', 'Because maize only grows in cold places', 'Because the Northern Cape has too much rainfall', 'Because no one lives in the Northern Cape'], 0,
    'Maize needs moderate summer rainfall (500-800 mm per year) to grow well. The Free State, North West, and Mpumalanga receive enough summer rain, have flat land, and have suitable soils. The Northern Cape is much drier (semi-desert in many places) and would require expensive irrigation to grow maize on a large scale.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Food Security ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Food Security?\n\n**Food security** means that all people, at all times, have access to **enough safe, nutritious food** to live a healthy and active life.\n\n### The Four Pillars of Food Security\n\n| Pillar | Meaning | South African Example |\n|--------|---------|----------------------|\n| **Availability** | Enough food is produced or imported | SA produces enough food as a country, but not all people can access it |\n| **Access** | People can afford to buy food or grow their own | Many poor families cannot afford healthy food |\n| **Utilisation** | Food is prepared and eaten safely and provides proper nutrition | Some people eat cheap, unhealthy food (high in sugar and starch) because it is all they can afford |\n| **Stability** | Food supply is reliable over time, not disrupted by drought, floods, or price increases | Droughts (like 2015-2016) can cause food prices to rise sharply |\n\n### Food Security in South Africa\n\nSouth Africa is **food secure as a country** (we produce enough food overall), but millions of **households** are food insecure:\n\n- About **1 in 5 South African households** do not have enough food\n- **Poverty and unemployment** are the biggest reasons — people cannot afford to buy food\n- Rural areas and informal settlements are most affected\n- Children who do not get enough nutritious food may suffer from **malnutrition** and **stunting** (not growing properly)'),
  q(2, 'South Africa produces enough food overall, yet millions of households are food insecure. What is the main reason?',
    ['Poverty and unemployment — many people cannot afford to buy the food that is available', 'South Africa does not produce any food', 'There are no shops in South Africa', 'People choose not to eat'], 0,
    'South Africa has a paradox: the country is food secure at a national level (enough food is produced), but many households cannot afford to buy it. Poverty, unemployment, and inequality are the root causes. The problem is not food availability but food ACCESS — the food exists, but many people are too poor to purchase it.'),
  t(3, '## Threats to Food Security\n\nSeveral factors threaten South Africa\'s ability to feed its people.\n\n### Natural Threats\n\n| Threat | Impact |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Drought** | Less water means lower crop yields; livestock die; food prices rise. The 2015-2016 drought was the worst in decades |\n| **Floods** | Destroy crops, wash away topsoil, and damage farmland |\n| **Pests and diseases** | Locusts, fall armyworm, and crop diseases can destroy harvests |\n| **Climate change** | Changing rainfall patterns, more extreme weather, longer dry periods |\n| **Soil erosion** | Over-farming and overgrazing strip the topsoil, making land less productive |\n\n### Human-Made Threats\n\n| Threat | Impact |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Population growth** | More mouths to feed with the same amount of land |\n| **Urbanisation** | Farmland is converted to housing and industry |\n| **Food waste** | About one-third of all food produced globally is wasted |\n| **Land inequality** | Most commercial farmland is still owned by a small minority (legacy of apartheid) |\n| **Rising input costs** | Fuel, fertiliser, and seeds are becoming more expensive |\n| **Water scarcity** | SA is already a water-scarce country; competition for water between farming, industry, and households |\n| **Load shedding** | Power outages affect irrigation, cold storage, and food processing |'),
  fb(4, 'About one-___ of all food produced globally is wasted. The worst drought in decades hit South Africa in ___.',
    ['third', '2015-2016'],
    'Food waste is a major global problem. About one-third of all food produced (roughly 1.3 billion tonnes per year) is lost or wasted. In South Africa, the 2015-2016 drought was devastating — it was the worst in over 30 years, causing massive crop failures, livestock deaths, and sharp food price increases that hit the poorest households hardest.'),
  t(5, '## Solutions for Food Security\n\nThere are many ways South Africa is working to improve food security.\n\n### Government Programmes\n\n| Programme | What It Does |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **National School Nutrition Programme** | Provides free meals to over 9 million learners at school every day |\n| **Social grants** | Child support grants, old-age pensions, and disability grants help families buy food |\n| **Land reform** | Aims to redistribute farmland more fairly so more people can farm |\n| **Agricultural support** | Government provides seeds, tools, training, and starter packs to emerging farmers |\n\n### Community Solutions\n\n- **Community gardens** and **food gardens** at schools, clinics, and community centres\n- **Permaculture** — sustainable farming methods that work with nature\n- **Rainwater harvesting** — collecting rainwater for irrigation\n- **Composting** — turning food scraps into fertiliser\n- **Seed saving** — keeping seeds from one season to plant the next\n\n### What You Can Do\n\n- Start a **vegetable garden** at home or school\n- **Don\'t waste food** — eat what is on your plate\n- **Compost** food scraps instead of throwing them away\n- Support **local farmers** by buying at farmers\' markets\n- Learn about **healthy eating** and make good food choices'),
  q(6, 'How does the National School Nutrition Programme help with food security in South Africa?',
    ['It provides free meals to over 9 million learners at school every day, ensuring children from poor households get at least one nutritious meal', 'It teaches children how to cook fancy meals', 'It exports South African food to other countries', 'It only helps children in private schools'], 0,
    'The National School Nutrition Programme (NSNP) is one of South Africa\'s most important food security interventions. It provides a free meal every school day to over 9 million learners, mostly in poor communities. For many children, this is the only guaranteed nutritious meal they receive each day. The programme improves concentration, school attendance, and nutrition.'),
  t(7, '## Sustainable Farming Practices\n\nTo feed South Africa in the future, we need farming methods that produce food **without destroying the environment**. This is called **sustainable farming**.\n\n### Sustainable Practices\n\n| Practice | How It Helps |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Crop rotation** | Growing different crops in a field each season to prevent soil exhaustion |\n| **Organic farming** | Farming without chemical pesticides or fertilisers — using natural methods instead |\n| **Conservation tillage** | Reducing or stopping ploughing to keep the topsoil intact |\n| **Intercropping** | Growing two or more crops together (e.g., maize and beans) — beans add nitrogen to the soil |\n| **Composting** | Using decomposed organic matter as natural fertiliser |\n| **Drip irrigation** | Using water efficiently by dripping it directly to plant roots |\n| **Indigenous crops** | Growing traditional crops like sorghum, millet, and amadumbe that are adapted to local conditions |\n\n### Indigenous Crops of South Africa\n\n| Crop | Benefit |\n|------|----------|\n| **Sorghum** | Drought-resistant grain used for porridge and traditional beer |\n| **Amadumbe** (taro) | Nutritious root vegetable grown in KwaZulu-Natal |\n| **Cowpeas** | High in protein, enrich the soil with nitrogen |\n| **Moringa** | "Miracle tree" — leaves are highly nutritious |\n| **Rooibos** | Unique to SA — grown only in the Cederberg region |'),
  fb(8, 'Growing different crops in a field each season is called ___. ___ is a drought-resistant indigenous grain used for porridge.',
    ['crop rotation', 'Sorghum'],
    'Crop rotation prevents soil exhaustion because different crops use different nutrients. For example, planting beans after maize replaces the nitrogen that maize used up. Sorghum is an important indigenous crop in South Africa — it is more drought-resistant than maize and has been grown in Africa for thousands of years. It is used to make porridge, traditional beer, and flour.'),
  q(9, 'Why might indigenous crops like sorghum and cowpeas be important for South Africa\'s food future?',
    ['They are adapted to local conditions, drought-resistant, and nutritious — ideal for South Africa\'s challenging climate', 'They are more expensive than imported crops', 'They can only grow in Europe', 'They taste better than all other crops'], 0,
    'Indigenous crops have been grown in Africa for thousands of years and are well adapted to local soils and climates. Sorghum is more drought-resistant than maize. Cowpeas are high in protein and fix nitrogen in the soil. As climate change makes rainfall less reliable, these hardy, nutritious crops could play a vital role in feeding South Africa.'),
];

