// CHAPTER 7 (Geography – Term 4): Mining in South Africa
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Minerals and Mining in SA ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Mining?\n\n**Mining** is the process of extracting (removing) **minerals** and other valuable resources from the Earth.\n\n### What Are Minerals?\n\nMinerals are **natural, non-living substances** found in the Earth\'s crust. They include:\n- **Metals**: Gold, iron, platinum, copper, manganese\n- **Precious stones**: Diamonds\n- **Energy minerals**: Coal, uranium\n- **Industrial minerals**: Chromium, vanadium, limestone\n\n### Why Is Mining Important to South Africa?\n\nSouth Africa is one of the **richest mining countries in the world**. Mining has shaped the country\'s history, economy, and geography.\n\n| Contribution | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| **Economy** | Mining contributes about 8% of South Africa\'s GDP (Gross Domestic Product) |\n| **Jobs** | The mining industry employs about **450,000 people** directly, and millions more in related industries |\n| **Exports** | Minerals are one of SA\'s biggest exports, earning foreign currency |\n| **History** | The discovery of diamonds (1867) and gold (1886) transformed South Africa — cities like Johannesburg and Kimberley were built because of mining |\n| **Infrastructure** | Roads, railways, and towns were built to support mining operations |'),
  q(2, 'How did the discovery of gold and diamonds change South Africa?',
    ['It transformed the country — cities like Johannesburg and Kimberley were built, railways were constructed, and people migrated from all over to work in the mines', 'It had no effect on South Africa', 'It caused everyone to leave South Africa', 'It only affected the Western Cape'], 0,
    'The discovery of diamonds near Kimberley in 1867 and gold on the Witwatersrand in 1886 changed South Africa forever. Thousands of people rushed to the mining areas. Johannesburg grew from nothing into South Africa\'s largest city. Railways, roads, and towns were built. But mining also led to land dispossession, migrant labour, and exploitation of black workers.'),
  t(3, '## South Africa\'s Key Minerals\n\n### Where Are They Found?\n\n| Mineral | Where Mined | Main Uses |\n|---------|-------------|----------|\n| **Gold** | Witwatersrand (Gauteng), Free State | Jewellery, electronics, investment |\n| **Diamonds** | Kimberley (Northern Cape), Cullinan (Gauteng), Lichtenburg (North West), marine diamonds off the west coast | Jewellery, cutting and drilling tools |\n| **Coal** | Mpumalanga (mainly), KwaZulu-Natal, Limpopo | Electricity generation (Eskom), steel production, exports |\n| **Platinum** | Bushveld Complex (Limpopo, North West, Gauteng) — SA has about 80% of the world\'s platinum | Catalytic converters (cars), jewellery, electronics |\n| **Iron ore** | Sishen and Kathu (Northern Cape) | Steel production |\n| **Manganese** | Northern Cape (SA has about 80% of the world\'s manganese) | Steel production (makes it stronger) |\n| **Chromium** | Limpopo, North West | Stainless steel, chrome plating |\n| **Copper** | Limpopo (Phalaborwa) | Electrical wiring, pipes, electronics |\n\n### Fun Facts\n\n- The **Cullinan Diamond**, found in 1905 near Pretoria, is the **largest gem-quality diamond ever found** (3,106 carats uncut). The largest cut stones are in the British Crown Jewels\n- South Africa produces about **75% of the world\'s chrome** and **80% of the world\'s platinum**\n- The Witwatersrand Basin has produced about **one-third of all the gold ever mined in the world**'),
  fb(4, 'South Africa has about 80% of the world\'s ___. The largest gem-quality diamond ever found was the ___ Diamond, discovered in 1905.',
    ['platinum', 'Cullinan'],
    'South Africa\'s Bushveld Complex contains the world\'s largest known reserves of platinum group metals — about 80% of the global total. The Cullinan Diamond was found at the Premier Mine near Pretoria in 1905 and weighed an incredible 3,106 carats before being cut. The two largest stones cut from it are set in the British Crown Jewels.'),
  t(5, '## Types of Mining\n\nThere are two main methods of mining, depending on how deep the minerals are.\n\n### Surface Mining (Open-Cast / Open-Pit)\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **How it works** | The top layers of soil and rock (overburden) are removed to reach minerals near the surface |\n| **Used for** | Coal, iron ore, diamonds, manganese |\n| **Advantages** | Cheaper and safer than underground mining |\n| **Disadvantages** | Creates huge holes in the landscape, destroys vegetation, produces dust, uses lots of water |\n| **Example** | Sishen iron ore mine in the Northern Cape — one of the largest open-pit mines in the world |\n\n### Underground (Deep-Level) Mining\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **How it works** | Shafts and tunnels are dug deep underground to reach mineral deposits |\n| **Used for** | Gold, platinum, diamonds |\n| **Depth** | South African gold mines can reach depths of **4 km** below the surface — among the deepest in the world |\n| **Advantages** | Can reach minerals that are very deep |\n| **Disadvantages** | Extremely dangerous (rock falls, flooding, extreme heat), expensive, unhealthy for workers (lung diseases) |\n| **Example** | Mponeng gold mine in Gauteng — one of the deepest mines in the world (about 3.84 km deep) |'),
  q(6, 'Why is underground mining more dangerous than surface mining?',
    ['Because miners work deep underground where they face risks of rock falls, flooding, extreme heat, and lung diseases from dust', 'Because the sun is too bright underground', 'Because there are wild animals underground', 'Because underground miners do not wear safety equipment'], 0,
    'Underground mining, especially at the extreme depths found in South African gold mines (up to 4 km), is inherently dangerous. Miners face rock falls (seismic events), flooding, temperatures exceeding 50\u00b0C, and inhale dust that can cause silicosis and other lung diseases. Despite modern safety measures, mining accidents and occupational diseases remain serious problems.'),
  t(7, '## The Migrant Labour System\n\nMining in South Africa created a system of **migrant labour** that had a profound impact on families and communities.\n\n### How It Worked\n\n- Men from rural areas (and neighbouring countries like Mozambique, Lesotho, and Swaziland) travelled to the mines to work\n- They lived in **single-sex hostels** (compounds) near the mines\n- They were **separated from their families** for months or even years at a time\n- They sent money home to support their families (**remittances**)\n- Wages were **low**, and working conditions were **dangerous**\n\n### Impact on Families and Communities\n\n| Impact | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Broken families** | Fathers were away for long periods; children grew up without fathers |\n| **Rural poverty** | Young, strong men left the rural areas, weakening farming communities |\n| **Health** | Miners developed lung diseases (silicosis, TB) and brought these back to their communities |\n| **Hostels** | Overcrowded, single-sex hostels created social problems |\n| **Cultural change** | Traditional family and community structures were disrupted |\n\nThe migrant labour system was closely linked to **apartheid** — pass laws controlled where black workers could live and work. The effects are still felt today in many mining communities.'),
  fb(8, 'Miners who travelled from rural areas to work in the mines were called ___ labourers. They lived in single-sex ___.',
    ['migrant', 'hostels'],
    'The migrant labour system was one of the most harmful social consequences of mining in South Africa. Men were recruited from rural areas and neighbouring countries to work deep underground for low wages. They lived in crowded hostels away from their families, sometimes for years. This system, reinforced by apartheid pass laws, devastated families and rural communities.'),
  q(9, 'How did the migrant labour system affect rural communities in South Africa?',
    ['It weakened rural areas because young men left to work in mines, leaving behind broken families and struggling farming communities', 'It made rural communities very wealthy', 'It had no effect on rural communities', 'It improved farming in rural areas'], 0,
    'The migrant labour system drained rural areas of their youngest and strongest workers. Men left to earn wages in the mines, leaving women, children, and the elderly to manage farms and households alone. Families were broken apart. When miners returned, they often brought diseases like silicosis and tuberculosis. The social and economic damage to rural communities continues to this day.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Mining and the Environment ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Environmental Impact of Mining\n\nMining provides valuable resources and jobs, but it also causes **serious environmental damage**.\n\n### Negative Effects of Mining\n\n| Impact | Explanation |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Land destruction** | Open-pit mines create massive holes; underground mines cause sinkholes and land subsidence |\n| **Water pollution** | **Acid mine drainage (AMD)** — when rainwater mixes with exposed minerals, it creates acidic, toxic water that poisons rivers and groundwater |\n| **Air pollution** | Dust from mining operations causes respiratory diseases; coal burning releases greenhouse gases |\n| **Soil pollution** | Heavy metals and chemicals contaminate the soil around mines |\n| **Habitat destruction** | Mining destroys natural habitats, displaces wildlife, and removes vegetation |\n| **Noise pollution** | Blasting, drilling, and heavy machinery create constant noise |\n| **Mine dumps** | Large piles of waste rock and tailings (leftover material after minerals are extracted) scar the landscape |\n\n### Acid Mine Drainage — A Major Problem\n\nAcid mine drainage is one of the **most serious environmental problems** in South Africa:\n- Abandoned gold mines on the Witwatersrand are flooding with acidic water\n- This water seeps into rivers and groundwater, poisoning water sources\n- It contains heavy metals (lead, mercury, uranium) that are toxic to humans and animals\n- Treating AMD is **extremely expensive** and may need to continue for decades'),
  q(2, 'What is acid mine drainage and why is it a serious problem in South Africa?',
    ['It is toxic, acidic water that forms when rainwater reacts with exposed minerals in mines, poisoning rivers and groundwater — especially from abandoned gold mines on the Witwatersrand', 'It is a type of clean water produced by mining', 'It is acid rain that only falls on mines', 'It is drainage from acid factories, not mines'], 0,
    'Acid mine drainage (AMD) occurs when sulphide minerals exposed during mining react with water and oxygen to produce sulphuric acid. This acidic water dissolves heavy metals from the rock and flows into rivers and groundwater. In the Witwatersrand area, where gold mining has left numerous abandoned mines, AMD is a critical environmental crisis that threatens water supplies for millions of people.'),
  t(3, '## Mining and Communities\n\nMining affects the **communities** living near mines in both positive and negative ways.\n\n### Positive Effects\n\n| Benefit | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Jobs** | Mining companies employ local people |\n| **Infrastructure** | Roads, clinics, schools, and housing may be built for mining employees |\n| **Economic activity** | Shops, restaurants, and services develop around mining towns |\n| **Skills development** | Workers receive training and skills |\n| **Corporate social investment** | Mining companies are required by law to invest in local communities |\n\n### Negative Effects\n\n| Problem | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Displacement** | Communities may be forced to relocate when mines expand |\n| **Health problems** | Dust, polluted water, and noise affect residents\' health |\n| **Social problems** | Mining towns often have issues with alcohol abuse, crime, and overcrowding |\n| **Environmental damage** | Pollution affects agriculture and water supplies |\n| **Inequality** | Mining companies may profit hugely while local communities remain poor |\n| **Ghost towns** | When mines close, towns can collapse as there are no other economic activities |\n\n### The Marikana Tragedy\n\nIn **August 2012**, striking mine workers at the Marikana platinum mine in North West province were killed by police. **34 miners died** and 78 were wounded. This tragedy highlighted the poor conditions and low wages that many mine workers still face in South Africa.'),
  fb(4, 'In August 2012, ___ striking miners were killed by police at the ___ platinum mine in North West province.',
    ['34', 'Marikana'],
    'The Marikana massacre on 16 August 2012 was the single most lethal use of force by South African security forces against civilians since the end of apartheid. Platinum miners at Lonmin\'s Marikana mine were striking for better wages and living conditions. The tragedy brought attention to the continuing exploitation and poor conditions faced by mine workers in South Africa.'),
  t(5, '## Mining Laws and Regulations\n\nSouth Africa has laws to manage mining and protect the environment and communities.\n\n### Key Laws and Policies\n\n| Law/Policy | Purpose |\n|------------|----------|\n| **Mineral and Petroleum Resources Development Act (MPRDA, 2002)** | All minerals belong to the state (not the landowner); mining companies must apply for mining rights |\n| **Mining Charter** | Requires mining companies to transform — increase black ownership, employ more local people, invest in communities |\n| **National Environmental Management Act (NEMA)** | Requires Environmental Impact Assessments (EIAs) before mining can begin |\n| **Social and Labour Plans (SLPs)** | Mining companies must submit plans showing how they will benefit local communities (housing, education, healthcare) |\n| **Mine Health and Safety Act** | Sets rules for worker safety underground |\n\n### Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA)\n\nBefore a mine can open, the company must conduct an **EIA** to study:\n- How the mine will affect the **land, water, air, plants, and animals**\n- How it will affect **local communities**\n- What the company will do to **reduce** negative impacts (mitigation)\n- How the land will be **rehabilitated** (restored) after the mine closes'),
  q(6, 'Why must mining companies complete an Environmental Impact Assessment before opening a mine?',
    ['To study and plan for the effects on land, water, air, wildlife, and communities — and to reduce and repair environmental damage', 'Because they want to find more minerals', 'To increase their profits', 'Because they need a holiday before mining starts'], 0,
    'An Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) is required by law to ensure mining companies understand and plan for the environmental and social effects of their operations. It forces companies to consider mitigation measures (how to reduce damage) and rehabilitation plans (how to restore the land after mining ends). Without EIAs, mining companies could cause unchecked environmental destruction.'),
  t(7, '## Mine Rehabilitation and the Future\n\n### What Is Mine Rehabilitation?\n\n**Mine rehabilitation** means restoring mined land to a usable state after mining ends. This includes:\n\n| Step | Action |\n|------|--------|\n| **Reshaping the land** | Filling in open pits, levelling mine dumps |\n| **Replacing topsoil** | Putting fertile soil back on top |\n| **Planting vegetation** | Grass, trees, and other plants to stabilise the soil and restore habitats |\n| **Water treatment** | Treating acid mine drainage and contaminated water |\n| **Monitoring** | Ongoing checks to ensure the land is recovering |\n\n### The Problem of Abandoned Mines\n\nSouth Africa has about **6,000 abandoned mines** where companies closed down without rehabilitating the land. These abandoned mines:\n- Leak acid mine drainage into water sources\n- Are dangerous — children and animals fall into open shafts\n- Create dust storms from exposed mine tailings\n- Cost the government billions of rands to clean up\n\n### The Future of Mining in South Africa\n\n- **Renewable energy minerals**: Global demand is growing for platinum, manganese, and other minerals used in batteries and green technology\n- **Responsible mining**: Greater pressure on companies to mine sustainably\n- **Beneficiation**: Processing raw minerals in South Africa (making jewellery, steel, etc.) rather than exporting raw materials — this creates more jobs and value\n- **Technology**: Drones, automation, and AI are making mining safer and more efficient'),
  fb(8, 'South Africa has about ___ abandoned mines that were never rehabilitated. Processing raw minerals in South Africa instead of exporting them is called ___.',
    ['6,000', 'beneficiation'],
    'The roughly 6,000 abandoned mines across South Africa are a massive environmental and safety problem. Companies that once operated these mines closed down without restoring the land, leaving toxic water, open shafts, and polluted soil. Beneficiation — processing minerals into finished products locally — is important because it adds value and creates more jobs than simply exporting raw minerals.'),
  q(9, 'Why is beneficiation important for South Africa\'s economy?',
    ['It creates more jobs and adds more value by processing raw minerals into finished products in South Africa instead of exporting unprocessed materials', 'It means closing all mines immediately', 'It means importing more minerals from other countries', 'It means only mining diamonds'], 0,
    'When South Africa exports raw minerals (like iron ore), most of the value-adding (turning it into steel, cars, or machines) happens in other countries. Beneficiation means doing that processing locally — for example, turning platinum into catalytic converters or iron ore into steel in South African factories. This creates many more jobs, develops skills, and keeps more of the wealth inside the country.'),
  t(10, '## Summary: Mining in South Africa\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- South Africa is one of the **richest mining countries** in the world, with vast reserves of gold, diamonds, coal, platinum, iron, and manganese\n- Mining has shaped South Africa\'s **history, economy, and geography** — cities like Johannesburg exist because of gold mining\n- There are two main types: **surface mining** (open-pit) and **underground mining** (deep-level)\n- Mining has caused serious **environmental problems**: acid mine drainage, land destruction, water and air pollution\n- The **migrant labour system** devastated families and rural communities\n- Laws like the **MPRDA, Mining Charter**, and **NEMA** aim to regulate mining and protect people and the environment\n- The future of mining depends on **responsible practices, beneficiation**, and adapting to the global demand for **green technology minerals**\n\nMining is both a **blessing and a curse** for South Africa. The challenge is to use our mineral wealth in ways that benefit all South Africans while protecting the environment for future generations.'),
];

