// --- Lesson 1: How People Communicated in the Past ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Communication?\n\n**Communication** is the process of sharing information, ideas, feelings, or messages between people.\n\nCommunication can be:\n- **Verbal** — using spoken words (talking, singing)\n- **Written** — using writing (letters, books, emails)\n- **Non-verbal** — using body language, gestures, facial expressions\n- **Visual** — using images, symbols, or signals (flags, smoke signals, traffic lights)\n\n### Why Is Communication Important?\n\nCommunication allows people to:\n- Share **knowledge** and **ideas**\n- **Trade** and do business\n- **Govern** and make laws\n- Express **feelings** and build **relationships**\n- **Record history** for future generations\n- **Warn** others of danger\n\nThe history of communication is a story of human **innovation** — always finding better, faster ways to share information.'),
  q(2, 'Which of these is an example of non-verbal communication?',
    ['Using body language and facial expressions to share meaning without speaking', 'Writing a letter to a friend', 'Sending a text message on a phone', 'Reading a book out loud'], 0,
    'Non-verbal communication includes all the ways we communicate without words: facial expressions (smiling, frowning), body language (folded arms, leaning forward), gestures (waving, pointing), and signals (traffic lights, sign language). Some researchers believe that up to 70% of human communication is actually non-verbal.'),
  t(3, '## Early Methods of Communication\n\nBefore writing, telephones, or the internet, people found creative ways to send messages over long distances.\n\n### Ancient Communication Methods\n\n| Method | How It Worked | Who Used It |\n|--------|--------------|-------------|\n| **Smoke signals** | Fires were lit and blankets used to control puffs of smoke; different patterns had different meanings | Indigenous peoples in Africa, North America, China |\n| **Drum signals** | Messages were sent over long distances using drums; different rhythms had different meanings | Many African societies used talking drums |\n| **Messenger runners** | A person carried a message by foot from one place to another | Used everywhere; the Zulu kingdom had a system of swift runners |\n| **Carrier pigeons** | Trained pigeons carried small written messages tied to their legs | Used in Egypt, Persia, and Europe for thousands of years |\n| **Horns and trumpets** | Blown to signal warnings, gather people, or announce arrivals | The kudu horn (vuvuzela ancestor) in southern Africa |\n| **Fire beacons** | Fires lit on hilltops to warn of danger or signal news | Used in southern Africa, ancient Greece, and medieval Europe |\n| **Cave paintings and rock art** | Images on rock surfaces communicated stories, beliefs, and events | The San people of southern Africa (among the oldest known) |\n\n### The Talking Drum\n\nThe **talking drum** is one of Africa\'s most important communication technologies. In West and Central Africa, skilled drummers could send complex messages over distances of **several kilometres**. The drums mimicked the **tonal patterns** of the local language — listeners could "understand" the drum as if it were speaking.'),
  fb(4, 'The ___ drum mimicked the tonal patterns of language and could send messages over several kilometres. The San people communicated stories through ___ art on rock surfaces.',
    ['talking', 'rock'],
    'The talking drum is a remarkable African invention. Skilled drummers could vary the pitch and rhythm to mimic the tones of their language, effectively "speaking" through the drum. Messages could be relayed from village to village across great distances. San rock art, some of which is thousands of years old, communicated spiritual beliefs, hunting stories, and cultural knowledge across generations.'),
  t(5, '## The Invention of Writing\n\n**Writing** was one of the most important inventions in human history. It allowed people to **record information permanently** and share it across time and distance.\n\n### Timeline of Writing\n\n| Date | Development | Where |\n|------|-------------|-------|\n| **About 3300 BCE** | **Cuneiform** — wedge-shaped marks pressed into clay tablets | Mesopotamia (modern Iraq) |\n| **About 3100 BCE** | **Hieroglyphics** — picture symbols on papyrus and stone | Egypt |\n| **About 1600 BCE** | **Chinese characters** — still used today | China |\n| **About 1000 BCE** | **Phoenician alphabet** — the ancestor of the Greek, Latin, and Arabic alphabets | Phoenicia (modern Lebanon) |\n| **About 105 CE** | **Paper** invented | China |\n| **1440s CE** | **Printing press** invented by Johannes Gutenberg | Germany |\n\n### Impact of Writing\n\n- Laws could be **written down** and applied consistently\n- History could be **recorded** and remembered\n- Knowledge could be **shared** across generations\n- Trade became easier with written **contracts and records**\n- Religion was spread through **written texts** (the Bible, the Quran)\n\n### The Printing Press\n\nGutenberg\'s **printing press** (1440s) was revolutionary:\n- Before the printing press, books were copied **by hand** — slow and expensive\n- The printing press could produce **hundreds of copies** quickly and cheaply\n- Books became available to ordinary people, not just the rich and powerful\n- This led to a **revolution in education, science, and religion**'),
  q(6, 'Why was the invention of the printing press so revolutionary?',
    ['It allowed books to be produced quickly and cheaply so that ordinary people, not just the wealthy, could access knowledge', 'It was used to print money for the first time', 'It made handwriting unnecessary', 'It was invented by an African king'], 0,
    'Before Gutenberg\'s printing press (1440s), every book had to be copied by hand — a slow, expensive process. Only the very wealthy or the Church could afford books. The printing press made it possible to print hundreds or thousands of copies, dramatically lowering the cost. This spread literacy, education, and new ideas (including the Reformation and the Scientific Revolution) across Europe.'),
  t(7, '## Communication in South Africa\'s Past\n\nSouth Africa has its own rich history of communication.\n\n### Communication in Pre-Colonial South Africa\n\n| Group | Methods |\n|-------|----------|\n| **San** | Rock art, storytelling around the fire, trance dance for spiritual communication |\n| **Khoikhoi** | Oral tradition, drums, fire signals |\n| **Zulu kingdom** | Messengers (izinduna), praise poets (imbongi), drums, smoke signals. King Shaka had a network of swift runners to carry messages |\n| **Xhosa, Sotho, Tswana** | Oral tradition, storytelling, music, praise poetry |\n\n### Oral Tradition\n\n**Oral tradition** means passing knowledge, history, and culture from one generation to the next through **spoken word, stories, songs, and praise poetry** rather than writing.\n\n- Every community had **storytellers** and **praise singers** who knew the history of their people\n- Stories taught **morals, values, and practical knowledge**\n- Praise poetry celebrated leaders and important events\n- Oral tradition was **not inferior to writing** — it was a sophisticated system that preserved knowledge for centuries\n\n### The Arrival of Writing\n\n- European missionaries in the 1800s developed **written forms** of African languages (Zulu, Xhosa, Sotho, etc.)\n- The first newspapers in South Africa were published in the early 1800s\n- Many African leaders saw the value of literacy and **embraced education** while also keeping oral traditions alive'),
  fb(8, 'Passing knowledge from generation to generation through stories and songs rather than writing is called ___ tradition. Zulu praise poets are called ___.',
    ['oral', 'imbongi'],
    'Oral tradition was the primary method of preserving and transmitting knowledge in pre-literate societies across Africa. It was a sophisticated system — praise poets (imbongi in Zulu) could recite the genealogies, histories, and achievements of their people from memory. Oral tradition is still valued in South African cultures today, alongside written communication.'),
  q(9, 'Why is it important to recognise oral tradition as a valid form of communication and record-keeping?',
    ['Because oral tradition preserved centuries of history, knowledge, and culture before writing arrived — it was sophisticated, not primitive', 'Because writing was never invented in Africa', 'Because oral tradition is better than all written records', 'Because only Africa used oral tradition'], 0,
    'Oral tradition has sometimes been dismissed as inferior to written records, but this is incorrect. African oral traditions preserved detailed genealogies, laws, histories, moral teachings, and practical knowledge for centuries. Praise poets could recite hundreds of years of history accurately. Recognising oral tradition as a legitimate knowledge system helps us value the intellectual heritage of African societies.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Modern Communication ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Communication Revolution\n\nOver the past 200 years, communication has been **completely transformed** by technology.\n\n### Timeline of Modern Communication\n\n| Year | Invention | Inventor | Impact |\n|------|-----------|----------|--------|\n| **1837** | **Telegraph** | Samuel Morse | First time messages could be sent instantly over long distances using electrical signals (Morse code) |\n| **1876** | **Telephone** | Alexander Graham Bell | People could talk to each other over long distances in real time |\n| **1895** | **Radio** | Guglielmo Marconi | Messages and music could be broadcast wirelessly to millions |\n| **1926** | **Television** | John Logie Baird (first demo) | Moving pictures and sound could be broadcast into homes |\n| **1969** | **Internet (ARPANET)** | US military/universities | Computers could share information across a network |\n| **1992** | **SMS (text messaging)** | First SMS sent in December 1992 | Short written messages sent between mobile phones |\n| **1994** | **World Wide Web** | Tim Berners-Lee (invented in 1989, widely used from 1994) | Information accessible to anyone with a computer and internet connection |\n| **2004+** | **Social media** | Facebook (2004), YouTube (2005), Twitter (2006), WhatsApp (2009) | People can share messages, photos, and videos instantly with millions |\n| **2007** | **Smartphone** | Apple iPhone launched | A computer, phone, camera, and internet device in your pocket |'),
  q(2, 'Which invention first allowed people to send messages instantly over long distances?',
    ['The telegraph in 1837, which used electrical signals and Morse code to send messages along wires', 'The telephone in 1876', 'The internet in 1969', 'The smartphone in 2007'], 0,
    'The telegraph, developed by Samuel Morse in the 1830s, was the first technology to allow near-instant communication over long distances. Messages were sent as electrical pulses along wires, using a code of dots and dashes (Morse code). Before the telegraph, sending a message from Johannesburg to Cape Town would take weeks by horse; with the telegraph, it took minutes.'),
  t(3, '## How Communication Changed South Africa\n\nModern communication technology has had a **huge impact** on South Africa.\n\n### Positive Effects\n\n| Effect | Example |\n|--------|----------|\n| **Connecting people** | Mobile phones allow families separated by distance (e.g., migrant workers) to stay in touch |\n| **Access to information** | The internet gives South Africans access to news, education, and health information |\n| **Education** | Online learning, educational apps, and YouTube tutorials help learners study |\n| **Business** | E-commerce, online banking, and mobile money (like M-Pesa) create economic opportunities |\n| **Democracy** | Social media allows citizens to hold government accountable and organise protests |\n| **Healthcare** | Telemedicine connects patients in rural areas to doctors in cities |\n\n### South Africa\'s Digital Landscape\n\n| Fact | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **Mobile phones** | Over **90%** of South Africans have a mobile phone |\n| **Internet users** | About **72%** of the population uses the internet |\n| **Most popular platforms** | WhatsApp, Facebook, YouTube, TikTok, Instagram |\n| **Most popular messaging** | WhatsApp is used by almost all South African internet users |\n| **Digital divide** | Rural areas and poor communities have less access to internet and technology |'),
  fb(4, 'Over ___% of South Africans have a mobile phone. The gap between those who have access to technology and those who do not is called the digital ___.',
    ['90', 'divide'],
    'Mobile phone penetration in South Africa is very high — over 90% of the population has a mobile phone. However, the digital divide remains a serious issue. People in urban areas with higher incomes have much better access to smartphones, data, and fast internet than those in rural areas or informal settlements. This inequality affects access to education, information, and economic opportunities.'),
  t(5, '## The Digital Divide\n\nThe **digital divide** is the gap between people who have access to modern communication technology (computers, smartphones, internet) and those who do not.\n\n### The Digital Divide in South Africa\n\n| Who Has Access | Who Does Not |\n|---------------|---------------|\n| People in **cities and suburbs** | People in **rural areas and informal settlements** |\n| **Wealthier** households | **Poorer** households |\n| **Younger** people (digital natives) | Some **older** people who did not grow up with technology |\n| People with **education** | People with limited education |\n\n### Why Does the Digital Divide Matter?\n\n- **Education**: Learners without internet access cannot do online research or access e-learning\n- **Jobs**: Many jobs require computer skills and online applications\n- **Information**: People without internet miss important health, government, and community information\n- **Banking**: Online and mobile banking is becoming the norm — those without access are excluded\n\n### Closing the Divide\n\n- Government programmes to provide **free Wi-Fi** in public spaces\n- **Community libraries and computer centres** offering free internet\n- **Schools** receiving tablets and internet access\n- **Mobile data costs** being reduced (data prices in SA have been criticised as too high)\n- Organisations like **Ikamva Youth** and **Khan Academy** providing free online education'),
  q(6, 'Why is the digital divide a problem for education in South Africa?',
    ['Learners without internet access cannot do online research, access e-learning, or submit assignments digitally — putting them at a disadvantage', 'It means children spend too much time on their phones', 'It only affects teachers, not learners', 'It is not a problem — everyone in South Africa has internet'], 0,
    'The COVID-19 pandemic starkly revealed the digital divide in South African education. When schools closed, learners with internet access could continue learning online, while those without — often the poorest — fell further behind. The digital divide widens existing inequalities: wealthier learners access vast online resources, while poorer learners are limited to what is available in their immediate environment.'),
  t(7, '## Responsible Communication\n\nWith powerful communication tools comes **responsibility**.\n\n### Dangers of Modern Communication\n\n| Danger | Explanation |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Cyberbullying** | Using technology to bully, threaten, or humiliate someone — this can happen via WhatsApp, social media, or SMS |\n| **Misinformation/Fake news** | False or misleading information spread online — can cause panic, division, and harm |\n| **Online predators** | Dangerous people who use the internet to target children |\n| **Privacy** | Personal information shared online can be misused — identity theft, scams |\n| **Addiction** | Spending too much time on screens can harm mental health, sleep, and physical activity |\n| **Hate speech** | Using communication to spread racism, sexism, or other forms of hate is illegal in South Africa |\n\n### How to Communicate Responsibly\n\n- **Think before you post** — would you say it to someone\'s face?\n- **Check facts** before sharing news — is the source reliable?\n- **Protect your privacy** — don\'t share personal information (address, phone number, school) online\n- **Report cyberbullying** — tell a trusted adult\n- **Be kind** — treat people online the same way you would in person\n- **Balance screen time** — make time for face-to-face interaction, exercise, and reading\n- **Respect others** — do not spread hate speech or discriminatory messages'),
  fb(8, 'Using technology to bully or humiliate someone is called ___. False information spread online is called ___ news.',
    ['cyberbullying', 'fake'],
    'Cyberbullying is a growing problem in South Africa, especially among young people. It includes sending threatening messages, sharing embarrassing photos, or excluding someone online. Fake news (misinformation) is false information that looks real and is shared widely on social media. Both are harmful, and it is every person\'s responsibility to communicate kindly and check facts before sharing.'),
  q(9, 'Which of the following is the BEST way to check if a news story shared on WhatsApp is true?',
    ['Check whether the story is reported by reliable news sources (like news websites) before sharing it', 'If it has lots of shares, it must be true', 'If a friend sent it, it must be true', 'If it makes you angry, it must be true'], 0,
    'The best way to verify information is to check multiple reliable sources. If a story is only circulating on WhatsApp or social media and is not reported by established news organisations, it may be fake. Be especially careful with stories designed to make you emotional (angry, scared, outraged) — these are often deliberately crafted to spread quickly without people checking the facts.'),
  t(10, '## Summary: Communication Then and Now\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Communication has evolved from **smoke signals, drums, and rock art** to **smartphones, social media, and the internet**\n- Africa has a rich history of communication, including **talking drums, oral tradition, praise poetry**, and rock art\n- Key inventions like the **telegraph, telephone, radio, television**, and **internet** transformed how people communicate\n- South Africa is highly connected — over **90% have mobile phones** and about **72% use the internet**\n- The **digital divide** means not everyone has equal access to technology — this worsens inequality\n- With powerful communication tools comes **responsibility** — avoid cyberbullying, fake news, and hate speech\n- Communication technology continues to evolve — what new inventions might change communication in your lifetime?\n\n### Reflection\n\nFrom San rock art painted thousands of years ago to a WhatsApp message sent in seconds — the human desire to communicate, connect, and share stories has not changed. Only the tools have changed. Understanding how communication developed helps us appreciate both our heritage and the technology we use every day.'),
];

